package com.shahaabapps.core.content

/**
 * The AZ-204 episodes. Each one is drawn from its topic and says nothing the
 * topic does not: the same facts, the same examples, the same mistakes to
 * avoid, ending on the topic's own thing to try today. PodcastTest holds the
 * shape; whether a line is true is checked against the topic it came from.
 *
 * They are written for a beginner developer. Every Azure term is said in
 * plain words the first time it is heard, usually with an everyday picture
 * (the vault is a safe, a managed identity is a badge); Leo asks what a
 * beginner would ask; sentences are short, one idea a line; and the exam's
 * own phrasing is kept but flagged as such ("on the exam, if..."). Each one
 * shows when to use what with small, ordinary apps (a recipe app, a little
 * online shop), and closes with the exam's take-aways, each as the phrase a
 * question uses and the answer it points to, before the thing to try today.
 * Where the time will not hold every detail, a detail goes rather than the
 * explanation. Az204Boards draws each episode as it plays.
 */
internal object Az204Podcasts {
    private val appService = Podcast(
        "az-app-service",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about putting a web app on the internet without looking after a server. Here's a question to start. You've built a small website on your laptop. What does it take to keep it running for everyone, all day?"),
            leo("A computer that's always on, I suppose. Then updates, security patches, and someone to restart it when it crashes. [sigh] That sounds like a second job."),
            maya("[chuckle] It is. Azure App Service takes that job off you. You hand it your code, or a container, and it gives you a web address, HTTPS, scaling and patching. You never log into a virtual machine."),
            leo("What's a virtual machine, in plain words?"),
            maya("A computer in someone else's data centre, which you rent and then have to look after yourself. App Service hides it completely. Picture a very well-run bakery. You bring the cake, which is your app. The bakery owns the ovens, the cleaning, the electricity and the front door."),
            leo("So what am I actually paying for?"),
            maya("A shelf in that bakery. You choose how big the shelf is, and how many copies of your cake sit on it. And that split is the first thing the exam tests, because every app has two parts."),
            leo("Go on."),
            maya("Part one is the App Service plan. That's the shelf. It sets the region, which is where in the world it runs, the operating system, Windows or Linux, the pricing tier, and how many workers you get. A worker, or instance, is one machine running a copy of your app."),
            leo("And part two?"),
            maya("The web app. That's the cake. It holds your code, your app settings, your own domain name and its certificates, and your deployment slots, which we'll get to. Say you're building a small recipe blog. One plan, one web app, and you're done."),
            leo("Can two apps share one shelf?"),
            maya("They can. Picture a small online shop with a public store website, and a tiny admin site that only the owner uses. Both can sit on one plan, which is cheaper. But then they share its workers too."),
            leo("So if the store needs ten workers on a busy day, the admin site gets ten as well?"),
            maya("Exactly. The number of workers lives on the plan, not the app. So two apps that must grow differently need two plans. That's a classic exam question."),
            leo("You said pricing tier. What is that?"),
            maya("A price level, a bit like phone contracts. Free and Shared are for trying things out, on shared hardware, with no scaling. Basic gives you your own workers and custom domains, but you add workers by hand. Standard adds auto scale, deployment slots and daily backups. That's where most real apps live."),
            leo("And above Standard?"),
            maya("Premium gives more power, more slots and private networking. Isolated gives you a whole environment of your own, for strict compliance rules. A hobby project is fine on Free. A shop taking real orders usually wants Standard."),
            leo("What's a deployment slot, in plain words?"),
            maya("A second live copy of your app, with its own address, where you test a new version before swapping it in. And here's the trick. Slots and auto scale both start at Standard. So the cheapest tier with staging slots is Standard, not Basic."),
            leo("Got it. Now, my app needs a database password. Where does that go?"),
            maya("Not in your code, and not in a file you commit. You add it as an app setting in Azure. An app setting is a name and a value, and App Service hands it to your app as an environment variable when it starts."),
            leo("Environment variable?"),
            maya("A labelled value the computer gives a program as it starts, like a note left on your desk each morning. In .NET it simply turns up in your configuration. For example, a to-do app reads its database address from a setting. You change the value in the portal, with no code change."),
            leo("And when does the new value take effect?"),
            maya("Straight away, because saving an app setting restarts the app. [pause] So if your app sends reminders at a quarter past four, maybe don't change settings at a quarter past four."),
            leo("[laugh] Noted. And when something breaks, where do I look?"),
            maya("The logs. App Service can keep application logs, which is whatever your code prints, plus web server logs and failed request traces. Logging to the file system switches itself off after twelve hours, so it never fills the disk. And the log stream shows them live."),
            leo("Here's one I've hit. The app sits idle overnight, and the first visit every morning is painfully slow."),
            maya("That's because the platform unloaded your quiet app, and has to start it up again. The fix is Always On, which keeps the app loaded. It's offered from Basic upwards. Picture a booking site for a small dentist. Nobody visits at night, so the first patient each morning waits."),
            leo("Wouldn't a bigger worker help?"),
            maya("No. A bigger worker makes a running app faster, but it doesn't stop a sleeping one having to wake up. And a second slot won't help either. That's a copy for testing, not a warm-up."),
            leo("What do people usually get wrong?"),
            maya("Four things. Committing secrets in a settings file, instead of using app settings or a Key Vault reference. Expecting slots on Basic. Forgetting that a settings change restarts the app. And on Linux, a setting's name is read exactly as typed, capital letters and all."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four quick rules. If two apps must scale differently, pick two plans. If it asks for the cheapest tier with slots or auto scale, pick Standard. If a secret sits in the code, move it to an app setting. And if the first request after idle is slow, pick Always On."),
            leo("And if a question says I changed a setting and the app restarted?"),
            maya("That's expected, not a bug. Settings are read as the app starts, so saving one restarts it. [pause] That one surprises people."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Create a web app with the .NET 8 runtime, on Linux, on the Free tier. Add an app setting called Hello, with the value World, and save. Open Log stream and confirm the restart lines. Browse the URL to see the default page, then delete the resource group when you're done."),
            leo("Hello World. The classic. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val slotsAndScaling = Podcast(
        "az-slots-scaling",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about two things every live app needs. Shipping a new version without breaking anything, and coping when lots of people turn up at once. Here's a question to start. Your website has real users right now. How do you put a new version live without them noticing?"),
            leo("Upload it really fast and hope? [pause] Hmm. If it's broken, everyone sees it broken."),
            maya("[chuckle] And there's no quick way back. The answer is a deployment slot. A slot is a second live copy of your app, with its own web address. You put the new version there first."),
            leo("What's it for, in plain words?"),
            maya("A dress rehearsal. The main copy, called production, is the stage the audience sees. The staging slot is the rehearsal room next door. You test the new version there, and when it's ready, you swap the two."),
            leo("What does swap actually mean?"),
            maya("A swap exchanges which copy answers your real web address. First Azure warms up staging, by sending it a few requests. Then it switches the routing, so the staging copy starts answering production. Users never see a restart."),
            leo("Routing?"),
            maya("The signpost that decides which copy a visitor reaches. A swap moves the signpost, not the files. And the old version moves over to staging. Say you're building a small online shop, and the new checkout page turns out to be broken. You swap back, in seconds, with no redeploy."),
            leo("[laugh] An undo button for releases. But what about my settings? Do they move too?"),
            maya("Most of them do. General app settings and connection strings travel with the code. But you can mark a setting as a deployment slot setting. Then it's a sticky setting. A sticky setting stays with its slot during a swap."),
            leo("Why would I want one to stay?"),
            maya("Picture a recipe app with two databases, a real one and a test one. Production must keep using the real database after the swap. So you mark that connection string as sticky. If the new version should bring a new setting along, you leave it unmarked, and it moves."),
            leo("When do I mark it? After the first swap?"),
            maya("Before. If you mark it after, the first swap has already carried the staging value across. And some things always stay with the slot, like custom domain names, scale settings, IP restrictions and Always On."),
            leo("Can the swap happen on its own?"),
            maya("Yes. That's auto swap. It swaps automatically as soon as a deployment to the slot finishes, and it waits for the warm-up first. Which matters, because you should never swap with a cold staging slot."),
            leo("Can I try a new version on just some of my users first?"),
            maya("You can. Set Traffic percent on the staging slot, say to ten. One visitor in ten lands on the new version, and a cookie keeps them there, so they don't bounce between versions. If failed requests stay flat, set it back to zero and swap."),
            leo("Nice. Okay, part two. The crowd."),
            maya("There are two ways to grow. A worker is one machine running a copy of your app. Scale up means a bigger worker, with more processor and memory, usually on a higher pricing tier. Scale out means more workers behind the same web address, on the same tier."),
            leo("How do I pick?"),
            maya("Ask whether each request got heavier, or there are just more of them. For example, a photo app where each request now edits a huge image needs a bigger worker, so scale up. A blog whose visitors doubled, doing the same work each, needs more workers, so scale out."),
            leo("Any catch with scaling out?"),
            maya("One big one. Your app mustn't keep things like a shopping basket in its own memory. Each worker has its own memory, so users bounce between workers and lose their basket. Keep that kind of state somewhere shared, like a cache or a database."),
            leo("[sigh] That one has bitten me. Can scaling happen on its own too?"),
            maya("From the Standard tier, yes. That's auto scale, and it follows a scale rule. A scale rule is a condition that adds or removes workers. Processor above seventy percent for ten minutes, a queue longer than five hundred, or a schedule, like weekdays at eight."),
            leo("Where do those rules live? On the app?"),
            maya("On the plan, because the plan owns the workers. And always set a minimum, a maximum and a cool-down. A cool-down is the least time between two scale actions, so the rule doesn't flap."),
            leo("Flap?"),
            maya("Go out, come back in, go out again, like a door in the wind. That's also why scaling in should be gentler than scaling out. Out at seventy percent, in at thirty. The same number both ways makes it flap."),
            leo("When is a schedule better than a processor rule?"),
            maya("When you know the rush is coming. Picture a homework help site that gets busy every school day at four. A schedule rule adds a worker just before four and removes it at six. That's cheaper than reacting after the spike has already started."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four quick rules. If production must keep its own database after a swap, mark that setting as a slot setting. If it must swap as soon as a deployment finishes, pick auto swap. If traffic doubles with the same work per request, scale out. And if the rule keeps flapping, add a cool-down and a gap."),
            leo("And if a question asks what a swap really changes?"),
            maya("The routing, not the files. The warmed staging copy starts answering production, and you can swap back. [pause] Oh, and auto scale rules sit on the plan, not the app."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("You'll need a Standard plan, which the free credit covers for an hour. Add a slot named staging to a web app. Change the app title in staging only. Swap, and confirm production shows the new title. Then swap back, and delete the plan."),
            leo("Swap, check, swap back. Easy. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val functions = Podcast(
        "az-functions",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is Azure Functions. Here's a question to start. You want a bit of code to run every time someone uploads a photo. Do you really need a whole web app sitting there all day, waiting?"),
            leo("It feels like a lot of waiting for not much work. [pause] And paying for the waiting, too."),
            maya("[chuckle] Exactly. A function is a small piece of code that runs only when something happens. You pay per run, and there's no server to manage. When nothing happens, nothing runs."),
            leo("So what decides when it runs?"),
            maya("Its trigger. A trigger is the one event that starts the function, like a doorbell. And here's the rule the exam leans on. Every function has exactly one trigger. One doorbell, never two."),
            leo("What kinds of doorbell are there?"),
            maya("Quite a few. HTTP, when a web request arrives, for small APIs. A timer, on a schedule. A queue message, for background work. A new blob, which is a file in Azure storage. A change in a Cosmos DB database. And messages from Event Grid, Event Hubs or Service Bus."),
            leo("Give me an app for some of those."),
            maya("Say you're building a photo sharing app. When a picture is uploaded, a blob trigger starts a function that makes a small thumbnail. A contact form on a website? An HTTP trigger. A to-do app that sends a reminder every evening? A timer trigger."),
            leo("And if my function reads from one place and writes to another? Isn't that two triggers?"),
            maya("No, those are bindings. A binding is a ready-made connection to another service, so you don't write any connection code. An input binding hands you data. An output binding takes whatever you return and writes it somewhere."),
            leo("Like plugs instead of wiring."),
            maya("[laugh] That's it. For example, a function reads a Cosmos document and writes a message to a queue. That's one trigger, plus an input binding for the read, and an output binding for the write. The connection names come from app settings."),
            leo("Tell me about the timer. I hear the schedule format is odd."),
            maya("It is. Most CRON formats, which are a short code for a schedule, have five fields. Azure's timer uses six, and the first one is seconds. It's called NCRONTAB. Seconds, minutes, hours, day, month, weekday."),
            leo("[sigh] Of course it's six."),
            maya("So every five minutes is zero for the seconds, then star slash five for the minutes, then stars for the rest. Put the star slash five in the first field instead, and you've written every five seconds."),
            leo("Good trap. What does a real one look like?"),
            maya("The topic's example is a reminder sweep at four in the afternoon, Sunday to Thursday. The timer is the trigger. An input binding hands in today's sessions. An output binding drops the reminders on a queue. And a second function, triggered by that queue, sends the notifications."),
            leo("Two functions there. Do they live separately?"),
            maya("They sit together in one function app. A function app is the box you deploy, holding many functions that share the same plan, runtime and settings. Each function still has its own single trigger."),
            leo("You said plan. Which plans are there?"),
            maya("Three main ones. Consumption scales to zero, and you pay per run. The catch is a cold start. That's a delay while Azure loads your function before the first run after a quiet spell, like starting a cold car on a winter morning."),
            leo("Any other catch?"),
            maya("A single run is capped at ten minutes, five by default, and there's no private network. Picture a small newsletter tool that runs a few times a day. Consumption is perfect. It costs pennies, and nobody minds a short wait."),
            leo("And when the wait does matter?"),
            maya("Premium. It keeps instances pre-warmed, so there's no cold start. It runs for sixty minutes by default, or longer if you set it, and can join a private network. But you pay even when it's idle. Say your app takes payments and must answer instantly. That's Premium."),
            leo("And the third one?"),
            maya("Dedicated, which runs on an App Service plan you already have, with spare room. The cost is the plan, not the runs. One catch. A timer won't fire unless Always On is set, because the host goes to sleep when it's idle."),
            leo("[laugh] Asleep on the job."),
            maya("Right. Two files to know, too. The host file sets things for the whole function app, like logging and time-outs. The local settings file is only for running on your laptop. It's never deployed, and it holds secrets, so never commit it."),
            leo("What do people usually get wrong?"),
            maya("Writing a five-field CRON. Running a twenty-minute job on Consumption, where it's stopped at ten, so use Premium or Durable Functions instead. Committing the local settings file. And expecting a timer to fire on Dedicated without Always On."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four quick rules. If it asks how many triggers, the answer is one. If it must not have a cold start, or needs a private network, pick Premium. If it's the cheapest for a few runs a day, pick Consumption. And if a job runs for thirty minutes, rule out Consumption."),
            leo("And if a question shows a timer schedule?"),
            maya("Count the fields. Six, and the first is seconds. That's NCRONTAB. [pause] And reading or writing other services means bindings, not extra triggers."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Create a Consumption function app in the portal. Add an HTTP trigger function from the template, and call it from the browser with your name. Then add a timer function that runs every minute, and watch Monitor show the runs. Delete the resource group when you're done."),
            leo("A function that runs every minute. I'll try not to stare at it. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val durable = Podcast(
        "az-durable",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about Durable Functions, for work that has to wait. Here's a puzzle to start. Your app asks a manager to approve something, then waits three days for the answer. What is the waiting code doing for those three days?"),
            leo("Sleeping in memory, I'd guess. [pause] But you're asking, so that's probably wrong."),
            maya("[chuckle] It's not running at all. And when it wakes up, it starts again from the very top. That one fact explains almost everything today."),
            leo("Okay, back up. What's a Durable Function, in plain words?"),
            maya("First, a plain function. It's a small piece of code in the cloud that wakes up, does one short job, and forgets everything. Durable Functions let you write a whole workflow, a list of steps, that can run for hours or days, remember where it is, and survive a restart."),
            leo("So a function with a memory. What are the moving parts?"),
            maya("Three kinds of function. Think of a restaurant. The client function is the waiter. It takes your order and hands it to the kitchen. In code, it starts the workflow and gets back an instance id, which is like a ticket number."),
            leo("And the kitchen?"),
            maya("The head chef is the orchestrator. That's the workflow itself. It decides the order of the steps, waits for each one, and remembers where it is. The cooks are the activities. An activity is one piece of real work, like calling another service, saving a row, or sending an email."),
            leo("So the chef never actually cooks?"),
            maya("Never. All the real work happens in activities. Say you're building a small online shop. Every order goes through three steps. Check the order, charge the card, email a receipt. Each step is an activity, and the orchestrator calls them one after another."),
            leo("And where does the chef keep its notes?"),
            maya("In the task hub. That's a set of storage tables and queues, a notebook in the cloud where the workflow's progress is written down. The list of what already happened is called the history."),
            leo("Okay. Back to the puzzle. What happens after the three days?"),
            maya("Each time the orchestrator wakes up, it replays. That means it runs its code again from line one. But for every step that already finished, it reads the answer from the history instead of doing the work again. So it races through the old steps in a millisecond, and carries on where it stopped."),
            leo("That's clever. But I can see a trap coming."),
            maya("The trap is that the orchestrator must be deterministic. That means it makes exactly the same decisions every time it replays. So no random numbers, no reading the clock, and no calls to other services directly."),
            leo("Why not the clock?"),
            maya("Because the time is different on every replay, so the code could take a different path, and the history wouldn't match. You'd get a non-determinism error. A brand-new random id, a GUID, has the same problem. Instead, the context, the object your workflow is handed, gives you a safe clock, called CurrentUtcDateTime."),
            leo("And how do I wait? Can I just make it sleep?"),
            maya("No. Thread.Sleep and Task.Delay aren't written in the history, so on replay the wait would start all over again. Use a durable timer, made from the context with CreateTimer. It's the only safe way for an orchestrator to wait."),
            leo("What about charging the card? Could the orchestrator just do that itself?"),
            maya("Never there. It would run again on every replay. [pause] And one more thing. An activity can also run more than once. So make it idempotent. That's a fancy word meaning that running it twice does no extra harm."),
            leo("[laugh] Charging a customer twice would be a bad look."),
            maya("It would. Now, the exam names five patterns, and you can tell them by their shape. The shop order we just saw is function chaining. A, then B, then C, each step using the last result."),
            leo("Next?"),
            maya("Fan-out, fan-in. For example, a photo app where someone uploads a hundred holiday photos. Start a resize activity for every photo at the same time, wait for all of them with Task.WhenAll, then build one gallery from the results."),
            leo("Fan out to many cooks, fan back in to one plate. And the third?"),
            maya("The async HTTP API. Picture a small accounting app where a report takes ten minutes to build. The caller gets an answer straight away, plus a status address to check on later. The work carries on in the background."),
            leo("And the last two?"),
            maya("Monitor is a loop with a durable timer between checks, like looking at a job every hour until it's done. And human interaction means waiting for a person. Picture a holiday request app. The orchestrator waits for an external event, a named signal sent in from outside, like Approved."),
            leo("[sigh] But what if the manager never answers?"),
            maya("Then you race the external event against a durable timer. If the approval arrives first, grant the holiday. If three days pass first, the timer wins, and you escalate. [pause] The manager's app sends the event with a POST to an address the client handed back."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Two patterns first. If the question says wait for a person, with a time-out, pick human interaction: an external event raced against a durable timer. If it says process many items in parallel and combine them, pick fan-out, fan-in, with Task.WhenAll."),
            leo("And the code traps?"),
            maya("If the orchestrator needs the time, use the context's CurrentUtcDateTime, never DateTime.Now. If it needs to wait, use a durable timer, never Thread.Sleep or Task.Delay. And if a step calls a payment service or sends an email, it belongs in an activity."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("It runs on your laptop with Azurite, a local stand-in for Azure storage, so no Azure needed. Create a project from the Durable Functions orchestration template. Start it, and POST to the HttpStart endpoint. Open the status address from the response. Then add a thirty-second durable timer, and watch the status stay Running, then turn Completed."),
            leo("Thirty seconds of patience. I can manage that. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val containers = Podcast(
        "az-containers",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is containers on Azure. Here's a puzzle to start. You've built a new version of your app, and you want just one in ten users to try it, while everyone else stays on the old one. Which Azure container service can do that on its own?"),
            leo("No idea yet. [pause] And honestly, what's a container, in plain words?"),
            maya("A container is your app packed up with everything it needs to run, so it works the same on your laptop and in the cloud. Like a shipping container that fits on any ship. The packed-up blueprint is called an image. You build an image once, and run it as many containers as you like."),
            leo("So the image is the recipe, and a container is the dish?"),
            maya("[chuckle] Close enough. Azure gives you three services. A place to store images, a quick way to run one, and a managed home for running many. First, storing. That's Azure Container Registry, or ACR for short."),
            leo("What does it do, in plain words?"),
            maya("It's a private shelf for your images. You push images up to it, and other Azure services pull them down to run. It can even build the image for you in the cloud, with an ACR Task. So a laptop without Docker, the usual tool for building images, can still ship an app."),
            leo("[laugh] Good news for my laptop. Are there price levels?"),
            maya("Three tiers. Basic, with ten gigabytes, for learning and small teams. Standard, with a hundred, for most real apps. And Premium, with five hundred, which adds geo-replication. That means copies of your images kept in several regions of the world."),
            leo("So if the exam says geo-replication..."),
            maya("Premium, and only Premium. Starting on Basic and then needing geo-replication is a classic mistake. Okay, second service. Running one container, quickly. That's Azure Container Instances, or ACI."),
            leo("When would I use that?"),
            maya("When you just want something to run, right now, with no setup. It starts the moment you ask, and you pay by the second. Say you're building a small recipe app, and every night you want to export all the recipes to a file. That nightly job is a perfect fit for ACI."),
            leo("Can it run more than one container together?"),
            maya("Yes, as a container group. That's several containers sharing one machine, one network and the same storage, like flatmates sharing a flat. Then there's the restart policy, which says what happens when a container stops."),
            leo("What are the choices?"),
            maya("Always, for a service that should keep running. OnFailure, for a job that should retry if it crashes. And Never, for a one-shot task. So the nightly export, which should retry after a crash but not loop for ever once it succeeds, gets OnFailure."),
            leo("[sigh] An export running for ever sounds expensive."),
            maya("Very. Two more things for ACI. A container's disk is thrown away when it stops, so mount an Azure Files share, a shared network folder, for anything you want to keep. And pass secrets as secure environment variables, never baked into the image."),
            leo("Okay. Third service. Running many."),
            maya("Azure Container Apps. It's the managed home for microservices. That means an app split into several small services, each doing one job. Picture a food delivery app, with separate services for menus, orders and payments. Container Apps runs them all on Kubernetes underneath, but hides it from you."),
            leo("What's Kubernetes, quickly?"),
            maya("A popular system for running lots of containers across many machines. Powerful, but a lot of work. Container Apps gives you the good parts without the cluster. Scale to zero, HTTPS from the internet, and revisions."),
            leo("And what's a revision?"),
            maya("Every time you deploy, Container Apps makes a revision. It's a frozen snapshot of that version of your app, and it never changes. In multiple revision mode you can keep the old one and the new one running side by side, and split traffic between them by percentage."),
            leo("Oh. So that's the puzzle. Ninety percent on the old one, ten percent on the new."),
            maya("Exactly. Neither ACI nor the registry can do that. And rolling back is easy. Just point the traffic at the previous revision. The image is already in the registry, so nothing needs building. A rollback is a routing change, not a build."),
            leo("How does it scale, when lots of users show up?"),
            maya("With KEDA, the scaler underneath. It adds replicas, which are extra copies of your app, based on rules. HTTP traffic is the default, but it can also watch CPU, memory, or the length of a queue. You set a minimum and a maximum number of replicas."),
            leo("Why does the minimum matter?"),
            maya("A minimum of zero means you pay nothing when nobody's using it. Great for a hobby blog. But the first visitor after a quiet spell waits while it starts up. That's called a cold start. For example, a shop's checkout service should keep a minimum of one, so nobody waits."),
            leo("And how does the app get its image out of the registry? With a password?"),
            maya("No. With a managed identity, which is like an employee badge Azure gives your app, plus a role that lets it pull from the registry. No password stored anywhere. And full Kubernetes, called AKS, only when a question really demands full control."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Three to start. If it says run this container now, no orchestration, billed by the second, pick Container Instances. If it says microservices, scale to zero, revisions or a traffic split, pick Container Apps. If it says build an image without Docker, pick an ACR Task."),
            leo("And a few more?"),
            maya("If a job should retry only when it crashes, the restart policy is OnFailure. If it says geo-replication, pick the Premium registry. And if it says no cold starts, set the minimum replicas to one."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("It costs a few cents. Create a Basic registry. Build a hello-world Dockerfile into it with the registry's own build command. Create a container instance from that image with the restart policy Never, and read its logs to see the output. Then delete the resource group."),
            leo("Hello, world, from the cloud. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val apim = Podcast(
        "az-apim",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is API Management. Here's a puzzle to start. A caller is sending your API far too many requests, and you've told the front door to calm them down. What answer does that caller get back?"),
            leo("Go away? [pause] I'm guessing that's not the official wording."),
            maya("[chuckle] Not quite. We'll get there. First, an API, in plain words, is a set of addresses your app answers on, so other programs can ask it for data. API Management, or APIM, is one front door that sits in front of all your APIs."),
            leo("Why would I want a front door?"),
            maya("Say you're building a weather app, and partners want your forecasts. Instead of calling your servers directly, everyone goes through the front door. It checks who they are, limits how often they call, can change requests and replies, and gives partners a developer portal, a website with documentation and a try-it page."),
            leo("Who does the checking, exactly?"),
            maya("The gateway. Think of it as the doorman. It receives every call and runs your rules, which are called policies. Separately, there's the management side, where you set everything up."),
            leo("Okay. I keep tripping on products and subscriptions. What are they?"),
            maya("A product is a bundle of APIs you offer, with its own terms, a bit like a phone plan. For example, a fitness app might offer a Free product that anyone can use, and a Partner product that needs approval. A subscription gives a caller a pair of keys to one product."),
            leo("And where does the key go?"),
            maya("The caller sends the subscription key in a header, which is a label on the request, or in the address. But notice what it proves. It tells the front door who the caller is. It proves nothing to your back end. That's a separate step."),
            leo("[laugh] Two different handshakes."),
            maya("Exactly. Now tiers, which are price levels. Consumption is serverless, so you pay per call and it starts fast, but it has no developer portal and no private network. Developer is for testing only. Basic and Standard are for real apps. And Premium adds several regions, private networks, and the self-hosted gateway."),
            leo("Self-hosted gateway? In plain words?"),
            maya("The doorman, packed as a container, that you run on your own machines. If a question says the gateway must run on-premises, meaning in your own building, or in another cloud, that's the self-hosted gateway, and it needs Premium. Running in several regions at once is Premium too."),
            leo("Okay. Policies. Where the rules live."),
            maya("And where the exam lives. A policy is a small rule, written in XML, a tag-based text format. Policies run in four sections, in order. Inbound, before the call reaches your back end. Backend, the call itself. Outbound, on the way back. And on-error, when something breaks."),
            leo("Can I set rules at different levels?"),
            maya("Yes. Global, product, API, or one single operation, which is one address and method. And here's the trap. At a lower level there's a base element, which means, run the wider level's rules here too. Leave it out, and the global and product rules silently don't run."),
            leo("[sigh] I can see exactly how that happens."),
            maya("Now the pair everyone mixes up. Rate limit and quota. A rate limit stops bursts over a short window, say sixty calls a minute. Go over it and you get 429, Too Many Requests. That means slow down. And that's the answer to our puzzle."),
            leo("And quota?"),
            maya("A quota is a long-term allowance, say a thousand calls a month. Go over it and you get 403, Forbidden. That means you've had your share. For example, a ticket booking app that gets a rush of calls when sales open wants a rate limit. A partner on a monthly plan wants a quota."),
            leo("Slow down, versus go away. So I was half right."),
            maya("[chuckle] Half. And both come in by-key versions, so you can count per caller address, per subscription, or by any value you like."),
            leo("What other policies are worth knowing?"),
            maya("Validate JWT checks a sign-in token. IP filter allows or blocks addresses. Set header adds or removes labels. Caching keeps a reply for a few minutes. And mock response returns a pretend reply. Picture a mobile team that wants to build its screens before the back end exists. Mock response lets them start straight away."),
            leo("And if a rule needs a bit of logic?"),
            maya("Use a policy expression. That's a small piece of C sharp inside the policy, with a context object that hands you the request, the caller and their subscription. For example, it could send Android callers to a different back end."),
            leo("You said the back end needs protecting separately."),
            maya("Right. Otherwise callers just walk around the front door. Lock the back end so it only accepts calls from the gateway, with an IP restriction, a private network, or a certificate. And the gateway can sign in to the back end with a managed identity, a badge Azure gives it, so no password is stored."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four rules. If it says limit bursts, or answers 429, pick rate limit. If it says a monthly or daily allowance, or 403, pick quota. If the gateway must run on-premises or in another cloud, pick the self-hosted gateway, on Premium."),
            leo("And the fourth?"),
            maya("If parent policies stopped running, look for the missing base element. And if callers can skip the gateway, lock down the back end. [pause] Okay, that's five. Bonus rule."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("The Consumption tier is free for the first million calls a month. Create a Consumption API Management instance, and import the conference sample API. Add a rate limit of three calls per sixty seconds. Then call it four times from the test console, and read the 429 and the trace."),
            leo("Getting told to slow down, on purpose. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val eventGrid = Podcast(
        "az-eventgrid",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about reacting to things that happen. Here's a puzzle to start. Say you're building a small photo app. Every time someone uploads a picture, you want to make a little thumbnail of it. How does your code find out that a new picture just arrived?"),
            leo("It checks the storage every few seconds? [pause] Hmm. That's a lot of asking for mostly nothing."),
            maya("[chuckle] Exactly. That's called polling. Asking again and again, anything new? Today's service flips it around. Instead of you asking, it tells you. It's called Azure Event Grid."),
            leo("What is it, in plain words?"),
            maya("A postman for news. An event is a short note that says, this happened. A file was uploaded. A resource was deleted. A user finished a lesson. Event Grid carries those notes, the moment they happen, to whoever cares. That's called push. Nobody has to keep asking."),
            leo("Where do the notes come from?"),
            maya("From an event source. That might be a storage account, a resource group, Key Vault, or your own app. The source sends its notes to a topic. A topic is just the address where events are posted, like a noticeboard."),
            leo("Is there only one kind of topic?"),
            maya("Two to know. A system topic is one Azure makes for its own services, like the storage account in our photo app. A custom topic is one your own app publishes to. For example, a learning app could post lesson finished every time a student completes a lesson."),
            leo("And how does a note get from the noticeboard to my code?"),
            maya("Through an event subscription. That's a route from a topic to one handler, the code or service that reacts. A handler can be an Azure Function, a Logic App, a queue, or a webhook, which is just a web address that accepts the notes. In the photo app, the handler is a small Function that makes the thumbnail."),
            leo("Does my handler get every single note?"),
            maya("Only the ones you ask for. A subscription can filter by event type, like BlobCreated, which means a new file arrived. Or it can use a subject filter. The subject is the event's path, a bit like a file's address. You filter on how it begins, and how it ends."),
            leo("Give me an example."),
            maya("Back to the photo app. You only want pictures in the photos folder that end in dot PNG. So the subject begins with the photos container's path, and ends with dot PNG. A text file never reaches your code at all, so you never pay for it either."),
            leo("Better than checking at the top of my code, then."),
            maya("Much better. Now, a question for you. How many times does a note arrive?"),
            leo("Once, surely?"),
            maya("At least once. So, sometimes twice. If your handler doesn't answer, Event Grid tries again, for up to twenty-four hours, waiting a little longer each time. You can also set how many attempts it makes, and how long an event may live."),
            leo("[sigh] So my thumbnail could get made twice."),
            maya("It could, so your handler must be idempotent. That's a big word for a simple idea. Doing it twice gives the same result as doing it once. Pressing a lift button twice still calls one lift. Check the event id, and if you've done that one already, do nothing."),
            leo("And notes that never get through at all?"),
            maya("They can be dead-lettered. Dead-letter means the notes that could not be delivered are saved as files in a storage container, like a lost-property box. You look at them later, instead of losing them. A corrupted file that keeps making your Function fail ends up there."),
            leo("Can I point Event Grid at any web address I like?"),
            maya("Not straight away. First there's a validation handshake. Event Grid sends a code, and your webhook must send the same code back. It's like confirming your email address before a newsletter starts. An Azure Function with an Event Grid trigger does the handshake for you."),
            leo("Okay. Now the part that confuses everyone. Event Grid, Event Hubs, Service Bus. Which one when?"),
            maya("Event Grid is for single things that happened, which you react to. Event Hubs is for a huge, constant stream, like thousands of readings a second. Service Bus is for jobs and orders that must each be handled carefully."),
            leo("Simple apps, please."),
            maya("Picture a small online shop. A product photo is uploaded, so make a thumbnail. That's Event Grid. Every click on the site is recorded for analytics, millions of them. That's Event Hubs. An order is placed, and it must be charged and shipped exactly right. That's Service Bus."),
            leo("[laugh] So the shop needs all three."),
            maya("Plenty of shops do. And the classic mistake is using Event Grid for that flood of clicks. High-volume telemetry is Event Hubs' job. Event Grid is for single events, one at a time."),
            leo("What else do people get wrong?"),
            maya("Three things. Saying yes to the handshake without sending the code back, so the subscription never gets validated. Expecting exactly once, when it's at least once. And filtering inside the handler, when a subject filter would stop the delivery altogether."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four quick rules. If it says react when something happens, pick Event Grid. If only certain files or folders should fire, pick a subject filter, begins with or ends with. If undelivered events must be kept, pick dead-lettering to a storage container. If a handler sees duplicates, make it idempotent."),
            leo("And if a question is about a webhook?"),
            maya("Then the webhook must answer the validation handshake, by sending the code back. Or use a Function with an Event Grid trigger, which does it for you. [pause] And the good news, the first hundred thousand operations a month are free."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Create a storage account and an Event Grid trigger Function. Add a system topic subscription for BlobCreated. Upload a file, and read the event JSON in the Function log. Then add a subject suffix filter for PNG, upload a text file, and watch it get skipped."),
            leo("Skipped on purpose. Very satisfying. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val eventHubs = Podcast(
        "az-eventhubs",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about huge streams of data. Here's a puzzle to start. Say you're building a fitness app, and every phone sends its step count every few seconds. Thousands of phones, all day long. Where does all of that go?"),
            leo("Straight into a database? [pause] Hmm. Thousands of writes a second sounds painful."),
            maya("[chuckle] It does. You want something built to swallow a flood, and hold it while the rest of your app catches up. That's Azure Event Hubs."),
            leo("What is it, in plain words?"),
            maya("Think of a tape recorder that never stops. Millions of small events a second can pour in from many senders. Event Hubs records them, keeps them for days, and lets readers play them back at their own speed. Even more than once."),
            leo("More than once? I thought reading something took it away."),
            maya("In a queue, yes. Not here. Reading doesn't remove anything. Many readers can read the same stream, and a reader can rewind. That's the key difference from a queue. And if you've met Kafka, a popular open-source tool for streams, Event Hubs speaks its language too."),
            leo("How is it organised inside?"),
            maya("A namespace is the container, with its address and its tier. Inside it, an event hub is one stream. And each hub is split into partitions. A partition is like one lane on a motorway. Cars in a lane stay in order, and more lanes means more cars moving at once. The tier is the price level: Basic, Standard, Premium or Dedicated."),
            leo("So the whole hub is in order?"),
            maya("No, and that's a trap. Order holds only inside one partition. So if one phone's steps must stay in order, you send them with the same partition key, for example the user's id. The same key always lands in the same lane."),
            leo("Okay. Now, who reads all this?"),
            maya("Readers, through a consumer group. A consumer group is one reader's own view of the whole stream, with its own bookmark. Picture a fitness app with a live leaderboard, and a nightly report of steps per day. Each one gets its own consumer group, so each sees every event, without disturbing the other."),
            leo("Couldn't they just share one?"),
            maya("That's a classic mistake. Two apps on one consumer group steal each other's partitions, so each sees only part of the stream. Readers are separated by consumer group, never by partition. A partition is only ever a slice of the stream."),
            leo("[chuckle] So partitions are lanes, and consumer groups are viewers. What's the bookmark?"),
            maya("A checkpoint. It's a saved reading position, stored in blob storage. If a reader restarts, it carries on where it left off. Save it after a batch, not after every event, because each checkpoint is a storage write that you pay for."),
            leo("Do I have to write all that myself?"),
            maya("No. In code, the EventProcessorClient reads all the partitions, shares them out across your running copies of the app, and keeps the checkpoints for you. Or use an Azure Function with the Event Hub trigger, which does the whole lot. You just give it a storage connection for the checkpoints."),
            leo("And how do I make it take more?"),
            maya("Two dials. The first is the throughput unit, a slice of capacity. One unit is one megabyte a second in, and two out. Turn on auto-inflate and Azure adds units when it gets busy. But it never shrinks back."),
            leo("[sigh] Of course it doesn't."),
            maya("[laugh] The second dial is partitions. More lanes, more readers working side by side. You choose the count when you create the hub. And events are kept one to seven days on Standard, or up to ninety on Premium."),
            leo("What if I want every event saved for good?"),
            maya("Turn on Capture. It writes events in batches to Blob Storage or a data lake, as Avro files, with no code at all. Avro is just a compact file format for data. If a question says archive every event with no code, the answer is Capture. You set a time window, one to fifteen minutes, or a size window."),
            leo("Okay, the confusing bit. Event Hubs, Event Grid, Service Bus. Which one when?"),
            maya("Event Hubs is a stream. Lots of small readings, logs or clicks, kept and replayable. Event Grid is a single this-happened note, pushed to a handler. Service Bus is a job that must be handled carefully, one message at a time, with a place to put failures."),
            leo("Simple apps, please."),
            maya("For example, a smart thermostat company. Every thermostat sends the temperature each minute. That's Event Hubs. A new firmware file is uploaded to storage, and something should react to it. That's Event Grid. A customer orders a new thermostat, and that order must be processed once, properly. That's Service Bus."),
            leo("So if each message needs its own careful handling, and a dead-letter pile for failures..."),
            maya("That's Service Bus, not Event Hubs. Choosing Event Hubs there is one of the listed mistakes. The others are expecting order across the whole hub, and checkpointing on every single event."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four quick rules. If two apps must each read every event, give each its own consumer group. If one device's events must stay in order, use a partition key. If events must be archived to storage with no code, pick Capture. And if it says millions of events a second, telemetry or clickstreams, pick Event Hubs."),
            leo("And if a question says replay yesterday's events?"),
            maya("Retention, plus a new consumer group that starts from an earlier position. Remember, reading never deletes. [pause] That's the whole personality of Event Hubs."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Create a Basic namespace and a hub with two partitions. It costs about a cent an hour. Send ten events with the az eventhubs command line, or a small console app. Read them with the Functions Event Hub trigger, or the processor sample. Then check Metrics for incoming and outgoing messages."),
            leo("Ten little events, and I can read them twice if I like. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val serviceBus = Podcast(
        "az-servicebus",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about messages that must be handled. Service Bus, and its simpler cousin, Queue Storage. Here's a puzzle to start. Say you're building a small pizza shop app. A customer pays, and you need to send a receipt email. What if the email service is down at that very moment?"),
            leo("The customer just waits? [pause] Or the email is lost. Neither sounds great."),
            maya("[chuckle] Right. So instead, the app drops a message in a queue, and carries on. A message is a job to be done, like send this email. A worker picks it up when it can. If the worker is down, the message simply waits."),
            leo("What is a queue, in plain words?"),
            maya("Think of the order tickets on a kitchen rail. The till puts tickets on, the cooks take them off. Several cooks can share the rail, but each ticket goes to just one of them."),
            leo("And Service Bus is the rail?"),
            maya("Azure Service Bus is the professional kitchen version. Everything lives in a namespace, with a tier, which is Azure's word for a price level. Basic, Standard or Premium. Inside, you make queues, where one receiver takes each message, and topics. Premium adds dedicated capacity, and much bigger messages."),
            leo("What's a topic?"),
            maya("Publish once, and many get a copy. Each subscription on a topic is like its own mailbox. For example, in a learning app, when a quiz is passed, one message goes to the topic. The certificates subscription makes a PDF, and the analytics subscription counts it. Topics need the Standard tier or above."),
            leo("Can a subscription choose which messages it wants?"),
            maya("Yes, with rules. A SQL filter is flexible, like passed equals true. A correlation filter matches properties exactly, and it's cheaper. One trap. A new subscription starts with a default rule that matches everything. Leave it beside your own rule, and every message still gets in."),
            leo("[sigh] Sneaky. Now, how does a worker actually take a message?"),
            maya("This is the bit the exam loves. The default is PeekLock. The worker takes the message, but it's only locked, not removed, like a cook putting a hand on a ticket. If the cook faints, the lock runs out, and the ticket goes back on the rail."),
            leo("[laugh] Poor cook. And the other way?"),
            maya("ReceiveAndDelete. It's faster, but the ticket is torn up the moment you take it. If you crash, the message is gone. So for any work that can fail, use PeekLock."),
            leo("When the cook finishes, what then?"),
            maya("You settle the message, and there are four ways. Complete means done, remove it. Abandon means let go, so it's delivered again, and its delivery count goes up by one. Dead-letter means move it aside. And defer means keep it but skip it for now, to fetch later by its number."),
            leo("What's dead-letter, in plain words?"),
            maya("The dead-letter queue is a side pile for messages that could not be processed. Every queue and every subscription has one. If a message is broken and can never work, dead-letter it. Completing it would hide the failure. Abandoning it would just bring it back, again and again."),
            leo("Until when?"),
            maya("Until the max delivery count, ten tries by default, and then it's dead-lettered anyway. And please, actually read that pile. If nobody does, failures pile up silently."),
            leo("What if the cooking takes a long time?"),
            maya("The lock lasts thirty seconds by default, up to five minutes, and you can renew it. Work for longer without renewing, and another worker gets the same message. Two cooks, one pizza."),
            leo("And if things must happen in a strict order?"),
            maya("Use sessions. A session groups messages with the same session id, and one receiver handles them strictly in order. Picture a shop where each customer's order updates must run in sequence. Use the order id as the session id."),
            leo("Any other handy features?"),
            maya("Duplicate detection drops a repeated message id within a time window. Scheduled messages arrive at a time you choose. And transactions let you complete one message and send another, all or nothing. Auto-forwarding can also pass messages from a subscription straight into a queue."),
            leo("Okay. So what's Queue Storage for?"),
            maya("Huge, cheap, simple queues, inside a storage account. Messages are small, up to sixty-four kilobytes, but the queue can grow past eighty gigabytes, which is where Service Bus stops. For example, a photo app making thumbnails. Trivial work, huge volume, no fancy features needed."),
            leo("Does it have PeekLock too?"),
            maya("No. It has a visibility timeout. When you take a message, it's hidden for that long. When you're done, you delete it using the pop receipt, a little token that proves you're the one holding it. Don't delete it in time, and it becomes visible again."),
            leo("And how is all this different from Event Grid and Event Hubs?"),
            maya("Event Grid tells you that something happened. Event Hubs swallows a huge stream of readings. Service Bus carries jobs that must each be handled, with a dead-letter queue for failures. So in our pizza app, the receipt email is a Service Bus job. And the thumbnails for profile photos? A Storage queue is plenty."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four quick rules. If a message can never be processed, dead-letter it. If messages for one order must stay in order, use sessions. If the queue must grow past eighty gigabytes, or you need an audit log of every operation, pick Storage queues. And if one message must reach several subscribers, use a topic."),
            leo("And what points to Service Bus over Storage queues?"),
            maya("Duplicate detection, a first-in first-out guarantee, transactions, or publish and subscribe. All of those are Service Bus. [pause] And PeekLock is always the safe default."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Create a Standard namespace, a topic and two subscriptions. Add a SQL filter rule to one, and delete its default rule. Send two messages with different properties from Service Bus Explorer in the portal. Then peek each subscription, and the dead-letter queue, and see who got what."),
            leo("Two messages, two mailboxes. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val appInsights = Podcast(
        "az-appinsights",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about seeing what your app is doing once real people use it. Here's a puzzle to start. Your shop app works perfectly on your laptop. Then it goes live, and a customer says checkout is slow. How do you find out why?"),
            leo("Add a few print lines and look at the screen? [pause] Hmm, no. I can't see the server's screen. And by the time I look, it's over."),
            maya("[chuckle] Exactly. You need something watching all the time and writing everything down. It's called Application Insights."),
            leo("What is it, in plain words?"),
            maya("A flight recorder for your app. It's part of Azure Monitor, and it quietly records every request coming in, every call your app makes out, and every crash. Each recorded item is called telemetry. That's just a fancy word for measurements sent back from far away."),
            leo("What kinds of things does it record?"),
            maya("A request is someone calling your app, like loading a page. A dependency is your app calling something else, like a database. An exception is an error, a crash with its details. A trace is a log line your code writes. And on websites, page views too."),
            leo("Do I have to write code to get all that?"),
            maya("Not for the basics. On App Service, Functions or AKS, you can switch on auto-instrumentation in the portal. That means Azure plugs the recorder in for you, with no code change. Requests, dependencies and exceptions come for free."),
            leo("So what's the catch?"),
            maya("You don't get custom events. Those are your own business moments, like lesson finished, or order placed. For those you add the SDK, which is a small code library, and you give it the connection string."),
            leo("And what's a connection string here?"),
            maya("The address label on a parcel. It's a setting that tells your app where to send its telemetry. You may also see an older thing called the instrumentation key. Use the connection string. New regions require it."),
            leo("Tell me more about custom events."),
            maya("Say you're building a small language-learning app, and you want a chart of lessons finished per hour, split by topic. That's a custom event, sent with TrackEvent, with the topic attached as a property. Properties are text, for filtering. Measurements are numbers, for charts."),
            leo("Not a trace, then? Or an exception?"),
            maya("No. A trace is a plain log line, and an exception is an error. Counting business actions and splitting them by something is a custom event. And for a single number you watch over time, like how many cards are in a deck, there's TrackMetric."),
            leo("Okay. So where does all this data end up?"),
            maya("In a Log Analytics workspace. Think of it as a big filing cabinet, with one drawer for each kind of telemetry. Requests in one table, dependencies in another, exceptions in another. And you ask it questions with KQL."),
            leo("KQL. What is that, in plain words?"),
            maya("Kusto Query Language. It's a way of asking the filing cabinet a question, one small step at a time. You start with a table, then add steps. Where filters the rows. Summarize groups them and counts them. And render draws a chart."),
            leo("Give me one I'd actually type."),
            maya("Start with requests. Where success is false. Summarize the count, by result code. Now you can see how many requests failed, and with which error. That's the whole idea. Table, filter, group, chart."),
            leo("[laugh] Table, filter, group, chart. Even I can remember that."),
            maya("Now, back to the slow checkout. One click on checkout might make four database calls and then crash. That's six recorded items. What ties them together?"),
            leo("The time they happened?"),
            maya("Close, but no. It's the operation id. Every item from one request carries the same id, like a tracking number on a parcel. The end-to-end transaction view and the Application Map are built on it. So you can follow one slow request through three services."),
            leo("Doesn't recording everything cost a lot?"),
            maya("It can, which is why there's sampling. Adaptive sampling is the default in the dot net SDK. Under heavy load it keeps only a share of the items, but it keeps related items together, and it scales the counts back up."),
            leo("[sigh] Wait. So my request counts don't just drop?"),
            maya("No, they stay roughly right. That surprises a lot of people, and the exam knows it. For example, a busy photo-sharing app can cut its bill with sampling, and its charts still show about the right numbers."),
            leo("And if I want to watch while I deploy?"),
            maya("That's Live Metrics. A live dashboard of requests, failures and CPU, with about one second of delay. Picture a small food delivery app pushing a new version on a Friday. You keep Live Metrics open, and you watch for failures as the new version takes the traffic."),
            leo("Anything people often get wrong?"),
            maya("Four things. Logging personal data in custom properties, because telemetry is kept for ninety days and anyone with reader access can query it. Turning sampling off under heavy load and paying for every item. Using the instrumentation key alone. And looking in the wrong table, because errors written as plain log lines land in traces, not exceptions."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("If the question says cut the cost but keep the counts accurate, pick sampling. If it says follow a slow request through several services, pick the operation id and the end-to-end view. If it says watch a deployment live, pick Live Metrics."),
            leo("And counting business actions, like lessons finished per topic?"),
            maya("A custom event with TrackEvent, and the topic as a property. And remember, the basics come free with auto-instrumentation, everything lands in a Log Analytics workspace, and you ask it questions with KQL."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("There's a five gigabyte free monthly allowance. Create an Application Insights resource. Run any sample web app with its connection string. Make a few requests, and one that fails. Then, in Logs, run requests, summarize count, by result code."),
            leo("My very first KQL query. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val availability = Podcast(
        "az-availability",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about being told when your app breaks. Here's a puzzle to start. Your app goes down at three in the morning. Who finds out first?"),
            leo("[pause] Hmm. Probably an angry customer. By email. In capital letters."),
            maya("[chuckle] That's exactly what we want to avoid. Monitoring is only useful if somebody is told. So, three things. Availability tests, alerts, and autoscale."),
            leo("Start with availability tests. What are they, in plain words?"),
            maya("A robot customer. On a schedule, it visits your site from different places in the world and checks that it answers properly. The standard test sends a web request from up to sixteen regions. It checks the status code, the page content, and that the certificate behind the padlock is still valid."),
            leo("When would I use one?"),
            maya("Say you're building a small booking site for a hair salon. It's public, anyone can open it. A standard availability test is the default choice there. Every five minutes, from five regions, it checks that the home page loads."),
            leo("What if my site isn't public? Like an internal tool for staff."),
            maya("Then a standard test can't reach it, because it runs from Microsoft's public regions. Instead, you write your own little probe that runs inside the private network, often a function on a timer. It checks the endpoint, and reports the result with TrackAvailability."),
            leo("[chuckle] So the inside reports out."),
            maya("Exactly. Public, use a standard test. Private, report in from inside with TrackAvailability. And the old URL ping test is retired, so move those to standard tests."),
            leo("Okay. Now alerts. What's an alert rule?"),
            maya("A smoke detector. It watches a signal, and when a condition is met, it goes off. The signal can be a metric, which is a number measured over time, or a log query, or availability results."),
            leo("Are there different kinds?"),
            maya("A few. A metric alert compares a number against a threshold, a line you draw. For example, server response time above two seconds for five minutes. A log search alert runs a KQL query, a question to your logs, on a schedule. Like, more than ten errors of one type in fifteen minutes."),
            leo("And the others?"),
            maya("An activity log alert watches management actions, like someone deleting a resource. Smart detection spots unusual patterns on its own. And an availability alert fires when enough test locations fail, say three of five regions."),
            leo("So if a question says run a query on a schedule..."),
            maya("That's a log search alert, and only that. [pause] Two more details. Severity runs from zero, critical, to four, verbose. And a dynamic threshold learns what normal looks like for your app, instead of you picking a fixed number. It's handy for a site whose traffic rises and falls every day."),
            leo("Who actually gets told when it goes off?"),
            maya("Whoever is in the action group. Think of it as a phone tree. It's a reusable list of who to tell and how. An email, a text, a push to the Azure mobile app, or even some automation, like running a function."),
            leo("Why not just put my own email in each alert?"),
            maya("Because people change. Picture a small team running a food delivery app. Ten alert rules all point at one action group. When someone new joins the on-call rota, you update the group once, not ten rules."),
            leo("And during maintenance? I don't want my phone buzzing all night."),
            maya("Alert processing rules can mute the notifications during a maintenance window."),
            leo("[laugh] Good. Now autoscale."),
            maya("Autoscale adds or removes copies of your app, called instances, as the load changes. It lives in Azure Monitor, and works for App Service plans, virtual machine scale sets and more. You set a minimum, a maximum and a default count, plus rules."),
            leo("What do the rules look like?"),
            maya("A scale out rule adds instances, like CPU above seventy percent for ten minutes, add one. A scale in rule removes them, like CPU below thirty percent, remove one. A schedule rule says weekdays eight till six, keep at least three. And a queue rule can follow another resource, like more than five hundred Service Bus messages."),
            leo("Here's a trick one. Add one above seventy, remove one below seventy?"),
            maya("It flaps. The two rules meet at the same number, so they argue. Add, remove, add, remove. The fix is a gap between the thresholds, out at seventy and in at thirty, plus a cooldown. A cooldown is a pause after each scale action, before it checks again."),
            leo("[sigh] And if I only make a scale out rule?"),
            maya("Then it grows and never shrinks, and the bill keeps growing. Always pair it with a scale in rule. For example, a ticket-selling site that's busy on Friday night should shrink again on Monday. And remember, autoscale changes how many instances you have. Scaling up, to a bigger tier, is a manual change."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("If the endpoint is public, pick a standard availability test. If it's inside a private network, pick TrackAvailability from code running inside it. If the question says run a KQL query on a schedule, pick a log search alert. If it says a metric crosses a threshold, pick a metric alert."),
            leo("And for autoscale questions?"),
            maya("If the instance count flaps up and down, add a gap between the thresholds and a cooldown. If the plan never shrinks, add a scale in rule. And alerts go to an action group, never one person's inbox."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Alerts cost cents, and a few availability tests are free. Add a standard availability test to any public URL. Create an action group with your email. Create a metric alert on availability results below a hundred. Then stop the app, and wait for the email."),
            leo("Breaking my own app, on purpose, to get an email. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val redis = Podcast(
        "az-redis",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about speed. Here's a puzzle to start. Your app shows the same home page to thousands of people, and every single time, it asks the database to build that page from scratch. What's wrong with that?"),
            leo("[pause] It's doing the same slow work over and over? Like brewing a fresh pot of coffee for every cup."),
            maya("[chuckle] Exactly. The fix is to keep a pot ready. That's called a cache, and today's is Azure Cache for Redis."),
            leo("What is Redis, in plain words?"),
            maya("A notepad kept in memory, right next to your app. It's a key-value store. That means you save a value under a name, called the key, and you get it back by that name. Because it lives in memory, not on disk, reads take under a millisecond."),
            leo("What belongs in it?"),
            maya("Anything you compute often and change rarely. Say you're building a small news app. The top stories page is the same for everyone for a few minutes. That's perfect for the cache. Sessions, leaderboards and counters fit well too."),
            leo("So how does my app use it?"),
            maya("The usual pattern is called cache-aside. The app looks in the cache first. If the value is there, that's a hit, and it's done. If not, that's a miss. Then it reads the real database, puts the value in the cache, and returns it."),
            leo("Does the value stay in the cache forever?"),
            maya("No. You give it a TTL, which stands for time to live. It's a best-before date on the key. After, say, ten minutes, the key disappears, and the next read fetches a fresh copy."),
            leo("But what if the data changes before then?"),
            maya("Then you invalidate on write. When the data changes, delete the key, so the next read rebuilds it. For example, a profile page. A user changes their photo, you delete their cached profile, and nobody sees the old photo. The TTL is the safety net, not the plan."),
            leo("[laugh] The safety net, not the plan. I like that."),
            maya("There are two other patterns. Write-through means every write goes to the cache and the database together, so the cache is always current, but writes are a little slower. And a session store keeps each user's session in Redis instead of on one web server, so you can add servers freely."),
            leo("How does my code connect to it?"),
            maya("In the dot net client, with an object called the ConnectionMultiplexer. Think of it as the phone line to the cache. Here's a quick quiz. How many should a web app create?"),
            leo("One per request? That sounds tidy."),
            maya("[chuckle] That's the classic mistake. Just one, shared for the life of the app. It's expensive to build and made to be shared. One per request runs the cache out of connections. So you create it once, as a singleton, which means a single shared copy."),
            leo("And what can I actually store?"),
            maya("A few structures. A string, with an expiry, caches a value. A counter you increase handles rate limiting. A hash is an object with fields, like a cached user record. A list makes a simple queue. And a sorted set keeps its members in order by a score."),
            leo("When would I want a sorted set?"),
            maya("Picture a small fitness app with a weekly step-count leaderboard. Each user is a member, and their steps are the score. The sorted set keeps them in order, so the top ten comes back instantly. Not a string per user, and not a list."),
            leo("What happens when the cache fills up?"),
            maya("The eviction policy decides which keys get thrown out. The default, volatile LRU, throws out the least recently used keys that have a TTL first. So keys without an expiry are a mistake. Memory fills, and eviction picks for you."),
            leo("Can I trust Redis to keep my data?"),
            maya("No, and that's important. Redis is not a database. Assume anything in it can disappear, and always keep the slow path, the one that reads from the real store."),
            leo("[sigh] Good to know before I learn it the hard way. Are there different sizes?"),
            maya("There are tiers, which are price levels. Basic is one node with no SLA, that's the uptime promise, so it's for development and testing only. Standard is two copies with a ninety-nine point nine percent SLA, for production. Premium adds saving to disk, private networks, clustering and geo-replication."),
            leo("And above that?"),
            maya("Enterprise, which adds extra modules like RediSearch and RedisJSON, and active geo-replication, meaning writes in several regions at once. Enterprise Flash keeps very large caches on cheaper flash storage. A hobby app is fine on Basic while you test. A shop that must keep its cache inside a private network needs Premium."),
            leo("Anything else people get wrong?"),
            maya("Caching one user's data under a shared key, so people see each other's data. Choosing Basic for production. And keeping access keys in code. Use managed identity where it's supported, or keep the connection string in Key Vault."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("If the question asks how many connection objects, pick one shared ConnectionMultiplexer, never one per request. If it says leaderboard, pick a sorted set. If it says read the cache first and fill it on a miss, that's cache-aside, with a TTL, and delete the key on write."),
            leo("And the tiers?"),
            maya("If it must persist to disk or live inside a private network, Premium at least. If it says RediSearch, or active-active across regions, Enterprise. And Basic is never for production."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("A Basic C0 cache costs a few cents an hour. Create one, and open the portal Console. Run SET greeting hello with a sixty second expiry, then GET greeting. Wait a minute, and GET again. Then ZADD two streaks to a sorted set, and read them back highest first with ZREVRANGE, with their scores."),
            leo("A greeting that disappears. Very dramatic. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val cdn = Podcast(
        "az-cdn",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about making your website fast for people far away. Here's a puzzle to start. Your site's pictures live on one server in Europe. A visitor in Lahore opens the page, and it crawls. How do you make it quick for them, without moving your server?"),
            leo("Buy a faster server? [pause] No, wait. The problem is the distance, not the server."),
            maya("[chuckle] Right. So you bring copies of your files closer to them. That's what a content delivery network does. CDN for short."),
            leo("What is that, in plain words?"),
            maya("Think of a chain of corner shops. The factory is far away, but each shop keeps some stock near you. A CDN keeps copies of your static files, things like pictures, stylesheets and scripts, on servers close to your users. Those servers are called edges, or points of presence."),
            leo("And the factory?"),
            maya("That's the origin. The origin is where your real files live, for example a storage account. When an edge doesn't have a file yet, that's called a miss, and the edge fetches it from the origin once. After that, everyone nearby gets the edge's copy. That's a hit."),
            leo("So the first visitor in Doha waits a little, and everyone after them is fast."),
            maya("Exactly. In Azure, the product is called Azure Front Door. Front Door Standard is the global front entrance to your site. It does the caching, which just means keeping copies, plus HTTPS, routing and some basic firewall rules. It replaces the older Azure CDN from Microsoft."),
            leo("Is there a bigger version?"),
            maya("Front Door Premium. It adds managed firewall rules, and Private Link, a private road from Front Door to your origin. Say you're building a small photography portfolio. Standard is plenty. A bank's website that must lock down its origin needs Premium. And the older classic CDN profiles still show up in exam questions, with the same ideas."),
            leo("Okay. How long does a corner shop keep its stock?"),
            maya("That's the TTL, the time to live. It's how long an edge keeps its copy. It's set by a header your origin sends, called Cache-Control. A header is a little label attached to the file, like the best-before date on a carton of milk."),
            leo("[laugh] Best before Tuesday. And if there's no label?"),
            maya("Then Front Door uses a default, a day or a few days depending on the product. And you can change that with a caching rule. There are three. Honor origin obeys the label, and it's the default. Override sets your own TTL, for files that arrive with no label. And bypass cache never keeps a copy at all."),
            leo("When would I never want a copy?"),
            maya("For anything personal. Picture a small online shop. The product photos are the same for everyone, so cache them. But the page that shows your basket is different for each shopper. If the edge kept a copy of that, the next shopper could see someone else's basket. So that route gets bypass cache."),
            leo("[sigh] Seeing a stranger's basket. That's the nightmare."),
            maya("It's the worst mistake in this topic. Now, query strings. A query string is the bit after a question mark in a web address, like size equals large. Query string caching decides whether each different query string gets its own copy at the edge."),
            leo("What are the choices?"),
            maya("Ignore them, and there's one copy no matter what. Use them, and every unique address gets its own copy. Or include or exclude certain ones. For example, a weather app's map with size small and size large really needs two copies. But a tracking parameter that never changes the file? Ignore it, or you waste cache."),
            leo("Now the big one. I've changed a file, but users still see the old one. What do I do?"),
            maya("Two tools. First, a purge. A purge throws away the cached copy before its TTL ends, by path, or with a wildcard. It's the tool for an emergency. But it takes a few minutes, it's per path, and it's a request to every edge."),
            leo("And the second?"),
            maya("Give the file a new name. Put a short fingerprint of its content in the name, called a content hash. A new name is a new address, and no edge holds a copy of an address it has never seen. So it can't be stale, and no purge is needed."),
            leo("Can't be stale if it's brand new. Fair. Why not just set the TTL to one second?"),
            maya("Because then the edge goes back to the origin almost every time, and you've thrown away the point of a CDN. [pause] The topic's own example puts it together. TwoTracks serves help pages and lesson diagrams from a static website in storage, behind Front Door. Diagrams have hashed names. The help page has a five minute TTL. And the API bypasses the cache."),
            leo("What else do people get wrong?"),
            maya("Besides caching personal answers? Forgetting that a purge is slow and per path. Pointing the origin at the plain blob address instead of the static website address. And using query strings for parameters that never change, which fills the edge with useless copies."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four rules. If a file was updated but users still see the old one, purge the path now, and use versioned file names so it doesn't happen next time. If different users need different content under the same address, bypass the cache, or vary on the query string."),
            leo("And the other two?"),
            maya("If a question asks what sets how long an edge keeps a file, it's Cache-Control from the origin, unless a caching rule overrides it. And if it asks for the current CDN and global entry point, it's Front Door, with Premium for managed firewall rules and Private Link."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Enable a static website on a storage account. Create a Front Door Standard profile pointing at it. Load the page twice and check the cache header, a miss and then a hit. Then change the file, see the old one, purge, and see the new one. Front Door has an hourly fee, so delete the profile after."),
            leo("A miss, then a hit. Like my first attempts at the exam. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val identity = Podcast(
        "az-identity",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about signing people in. Here's a puzzle to start. Every night at two in the morning, a little job wakes up and reads everyone's calendar. Nobody is signed in. So whose permission is it using?"),
            leo("Mine? [pause] No, I'm asleep. Hmm. Nobody's, then?"),
            maya("[chuckle] Good instinct. We'll come back to it. First, the big picture. Microsoft has a sign-in service called the Microsoft identity platform. It sits behind Microsoft accounts and Entra ID. Entra ID is the new name for Azure Active Directory, the list of people and apps in an organisation."),
            leo("What does it do for me, in plain words?"),
            maya("Think of a hotel front desk. Your app doesn't check passports itself. Users go to the desk, sign in with Microsoft, and come back with proof. That proof is called a token. Say you're building a small team to-do app. You store no passwords at all. Microsoft does the signing in."),
            leo("How does the front desk know about my app?"),
            maya("You register it, once. Registering creates an application object, the single record of your app, in your home tenant. A tenant is one organisation's own space in Entra ID, like one company's building. Your app also gets an id, called the client id."),
            leo("And if another company uses my app?"),
            maya("Then their tenant gets a service principal. That's your app's local stand-in inside their building, like a visitor's pass from their own reception. Permissions and roles attach to the service principal. So, one application object, and one service principal in each tenant that uses the app."),
            leo("What goes into a registration?"),
            maya("A few settings. Supported account types, which is who can sign in, just your company or any company. A redirect URI, the address the tokens are sent back to, like the return address on an envelope. And API permissions, what your app wants to use, for example reading the user's profile from Microsoft Graph."),
            leo("And secrets?"),
            maya("A client secret or a certificate proves the app's own identity, like a password for the app itself. Only apps on a server get one, and a certificate is preferred. And if your app offers its own API, you list its scopes under Expose an API. A scope is one named thing an app may do, like read logs."),
            leo("Okay, tokens. You said proof. Is it one ticket?"),
            maya("Three. An ID token says who the user is. It's for your app, so you can show their name. An access token says what the caller may do on an API, and it's sent to that API. And a refresh token lets the app quietly get new tokens later, without asking the user again."),
            leo("So can my app read the access token to decide which buttons to show?"),
            maya("No, and that's a classic mistake. The access token is for the API, like a concert ticket for the door staff. Use the ID token for the user's name. And the API must check the audience in the token, meaning who it was issued for. A token for Microsoft Graph is not a token for you."),
            leo("[laugh] Someone else's concert ticket. Got it. Back to the puzzle, then."),
            maya("There are two kinds of permission, and the question is, is a person there? A delegated permission means a signed-in user is acting through the app. Like giving a friend your key to water your plants. They can only do what you could do. The user consents, or an admin consents for everyone."),
            leo("Consents?"),
            maya("Consent is saying yes on that screen that asks, this app wants to read your profile, allow? [pause] The other kind is an application permission. The app acts as itself, with no user at all. That's our nightly job. And an admin always has to consent to it."),
            leo("So the nightly job uses an application permission, with admin consent."),
            maya("Exactly. For example, in a to-do app where Sara reads her own tasks, that's delegated. A nightly report reading everyone's calendar is application. In the token, delegated shows up as the scope claim, and application shows up as the roles claim. Asking for application permissions in a normal user sign-in is the opposite mistake."),
            leo("You said only server apps get a secret. What about a phone app?"),
            maya("That's a public client: a mobile app, a desktop app, or a single-page app in the browser. Anything on a user's device can be taken apart, so it can't keep a secret. A confidential client is a web app or a background job on a server. It can keep a secret, and it can ask for application permissions."),
            leo("So no secrets in mobile apps."),
            maya("Never. Public clients use PKCE instead, Proof Key for Code Exchange. Picture a cloakroom. You get a ticket, and you also keep a secret word you made up. To collect your coat you need both. So even if someone copies your ticket on the way, it's useless to them."),
            leo("[sigh] Okay. And then there are flows. What's a flow?"),
            maya("A flow is just the steps an app follows to get a token. Five to know. Authorization code with PKCE, when a user signs in through a browser. It's the default for web, mobile and single-page apps. And client credentials, for a job with no user, using an application permission and a secret or certificate."),
            leo("And the other three?"),
            maya("On-behalf-of, when your API calls another API as the same user. Device code, when the device has no browser, like a TV or a command line tool, so the user types a code on their phone. And implicit, which is old and deprecated. Pick authorization code with PKCE instead."),
            leo("Can you put it all together in one app?"),
            maya("The topic's example. TwoTracks registers two apps. The mobile app is a public client with PKCE. The API exposes a Logs read-write scope. When Usaid signs in, he consents once, and gets an ID token for his name, plus access tokens for the API and for Graph. A nightly report uses client credentials."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four rules. If nobody is signed in, like a daemon or a nightly job, pick an application permission, admin consent, and client credentials. If a user works with their own data, pick delegated. If it's a mobile or single-page app, pick authorization code with PKCE, never implicit and never a secret."),
            leo("And the fourth?"),
            maya("If another company's tenant uses your app, a service principal is created there, and permissions attach to it. [pause] And two quick ones. An API calling another API as the user is on-behalf-of. And the API always checks the audience."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Any free Entra tenant works. Register an app with a localhost redirect URI. Note the client id and the tenant id. Add Microsoft Graph's User Read under API permissions. Then open Authentication, and compare the public client and web platform settings."),
            leo("My very own service principal. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val msal = Podcast(
        "az-msal",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Last time we met tokens, the proof your app gets when someone signs in. Today is about getting those tokens in code. Here's a puzzle to start. Your app asks for a token and gets an error called MsalUiRequiredException. What is it telling you?"),
            leo("The server's busy, try again? [pause] Hmm. That sounds too easy."),
            maya("[chuckle] It is too easy. We'll come back to it. First, the tool. It's called MSAL, which stands for Microsoft Authentication Library."),
            leo("What is it, in plain words?"),
            maya("A helper that talks to the Microsoft sign-in service for you. You tell it which app you are, and which scopes you need. A scope is one named permission, like reading the user's profile. MSAL shows the sign-in window, keeps tokens in a cache, which is a little memory, and renews them when they run out."),
            leo("So I never build token requests by hand."),
            maya("Never. You start from a builder, which is just the starting point in code. There are two, one for each kind of app. The public client builder is for apps on a user's device: mobile, desktop and command line apps. The confidential client builder is for apps on a server: web apps, APIs and background jobs."),
            leo("Why two?"),
            maya("Because a server can keep a secret, and a phone can't. Say you're building a small habit tracker for Android. The phone app is a public client, with no secret inside. Its API on the server is a confidential client. Putting a secret in a public client is one of the classic mistakes."),
            leo("Okay. So how do I actually ask for a token?"),
            maya("With methods that all start with acquire token. The first one to call, always, is AcquireTokenSilent. It looks in the cache and hands back a token with no prompt at all. If the token is old, it quietly uses the refresh token to get a new one."),
            leo("Like a coffee shop loyalty card. They already know me, so no forms."),
            maya("[laugh] Nice. Now, the puzzle. When silent can't help, it throws MsalUiRequiredException. The name does the work. UI means user interface. The user has to be asked something. Maybe they must agree to a permission, do a second check on their phone, or their session has expired."),
            leo("So I just retry silently?"),
            maya("No. Retrying silently can't change what's in the cache, so it just throws again. You fall back to AcquireTokenInteractive, which shows the sign-in window. So the pattern is silent first, and interactive only on that exception."),
            leo("What if I call interactive every time? Simpler."),
            maya("Then users see a sign-in window constantly. The exam loves showing that code and asking what's wrong. For example, picture a notes app that makes you sign in every time you save a note. Nobody keeps that app."),
            leo("[sigh] I'd delete it. How long does silent keep working?"),
            maya("For months. The topic's example is the TwoTracks Android app. On the very first run, silent throws, so it shows the Microsoft sign-in and saves the account. A month later the access token has long expired, but silent still works, because the refresh token fetches a new one. Only if that fails too is the user asked."),
            leo("And a job with nobody signed in at all?"),
            maya("Say your app is a small online shop with a nightly job that emails sales reports. No user. That's client credentials, where the app signs in as itself. On a confidential client you call AcquireTokenForClient. And the scope is the resource's address, followed by slash dot default."),
            leo("Dot default? What does that mean?"),
            maya("It means, give me every permission already approved for this app on that resource. With no user, there's nobody to approve anything narrower. So client credentials always uses dot default. Asking for User Read there just fails."),
            leo("Can I ask for Microsoft Graph and my own API in one call? Saves time."),
            maya("No, and that's a trap. Every access token has one audience, meaning the one API it was made for. Like a train ticket for one line only. So you ask for Graph scopes and your own API's scopes in separate calls. Scopes are written as the resource, then the permission, like Graph's User Read."),
            leo("I've also seen openid and profile. What are those?"),
            maya("Those are sign-in scopes from OpenID Connect, the standard for proving who someone is. And offline access is the one that gets you a refresh token."),
            leo("Any other methods I should know?"),
            maya("Three more. An API that calls another API as the same user uses AcquireTokenOnBehalfOf. A command line tool on a machine with no browser uses AcquireTokenWithDeviceCode, and the user types a code on their phone. And a web app signing users in uses AcquireTokenByAuthorizationCode."),
            leo("And a web app running on lots of servers?"),
            maya("Use Microsoft Identity Web, which wraps MSAL for dot NET web apps. And keep the token cache in a shared cache, like Redis or SQL. If each server keeps tokens in its own memory, they vanish when you add servers. That's the last mistake on the list."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four rules. If users complain about constant sign-in prompts, call AcquireTokenSilent first, and fall back to interactive only on MsalUiRequiredException. If a daemon with no user needs a token, pick AcquireTokenForClient with the dot default scope."),
            leo("And the other two?"),
            maya("If one request mixes scopes from two APIs and fails, it's because a token has a single audience. And if the machine has no browser, pick device code. [pause] Bonus: public client builder for apps with users, confidential builder for servers."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Use the app registration from last time. Create a dot NET console app, and add the MSAL package. Acquire a token for User Read interactively. Paste the access token into a token decoder, and read its audience and scope claims. Then run it again, and confirm the silent path succeeds."),
            leo("Silent success. My favourite kind. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val graph = Podcast(
        "az-graph",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is Microsoft Graph. Here's a puzzle to start. You have thirty separate requests to send to it. What's the fewest number of calls you can send them in? Hold that thought."),
            leo("Thirty? [pause] I'd guess one. But you said hold that thought, so probably not."),
            maya("[chuckle] Good instinct. We'll come back to it. First, what Microsoft Graph actually is."),
            leo("Yes please. What is it, in plain words?"),
            maya("It's one front door to Microsoft 365. Users, email, calendars, files and Teams, all behind one web address. Instead of learning a different service for each, your app knocks on one door and asks. It's a REST API, which just means you ask for things with ordinary web requests, like GET to read something."),
            leo("When would a small app actually use it?"),
            maya("Say you're building a small team dashboard. It shows each person's name and photo, and their next free slot in the calendar. All of that lives in Microsoft 365, so Graph is how you fetch it. The topic's own example is a study app showing a partner's photo and next free calendar slot."),
            leo("And how does Graph know I'm allowed to ask?"),
            maya("You send an access token with every request. Think of it as a ticket, with the name of the venue printed on it. That name is called the audience. For Graph, the ticket must say Graph."),
            leo("So I can't reuse the token for my own API?"),
            maya("No, and that's the first mistake on the list. One token has one audience. Your dashboard needs a Graph ticket for photos and calendars, and a separate ticket for its own API. Show the wrong ticket, and you're turned away at the door."),
            leo("And how do I know which permissions a call needs?"),
            maya("Permissions are called scopes, like User Read to see a profile, or Calendars Read for a calendar. To find the right one, use Graph Explorer. It's a web page where you sign in with a test account and try calls. Its permissions tab shows the scope, and you copy it into your app registration, your app's ID card in Azure."),
            leo("Okay, I'm in. How do I ask for exactly what I want?"),
            maya("With OData query parameters. They're little add-ons at the end of a request that shape the answer. Select says which fields you want, like just the name and email. Filter is a where clause, so only unread mail, say. Orderby sorts. And top sets how many come back in one page."),
            leo("Does select really matter? It sounds fussy."),
            maya("It matters. For example, a phone app that lists unread email only needs the subject and the sender. Ask for just those, and the answer is much smaller and faster. Without select, you get every field of every message. In Graph Explorer, get yourself, then do it again with select, and watch the answer shrink."),
            leo("[laugh] Like ordering one coffee, instead of the whole menu."),
            maya("Exactly. There are a few more. Expand pulls in something related, like a group's members. Count adds a total. And search does a full text search."),
            leo("Any catch with those?"),
            maya("A favourite exam detail. Count, search, and some filters on users and groups need an extra header, ConsistencyLevel: eventual. A header is a small note sent along with the request. Forget it, and those queries don't work."),
            leo("One tiny note. Okay. What if there are thousands of results?"),
            maya("They come in pages, like a long search result split over many screens. Each page that has more carries a next link. That's the full web address of the following page. You call it, and keep following, until a page arrives without one."),
            leo("So if my call returns only the first hundred users?"),
            maya("Follow the next link. Picture a company directory app that lists every employee. If it ignores the next link, it shows the first page and quietly misses everyone else. Reporting partial results like that is another mistake on the list."),
            leo("Right. Now, back to the puzzle. Thirty calls."),
            maya("Graph has a batch endpoint. A batch is one envelope with many requests inside. You post it once, and each request inside gets its own id and its own answer. But one batch carries at most twenty requests."),
            leo("[chuckle] So thirty is two batches, not one."),
            maya("Two. And that number is exactly what the exam asks. For example, a profile page needs the user's details, their photo and their manager. Three separate calls would work, but that's three round trips. One batch does it in one."),
            leo("What if Graph thinks I'm asking too much?"),
            maya("You get a 429. That's Graph saying slow down, you're being throttled. The answer carries a Retry-After header, telling you how long to wait. Honour it. The Graph SDKs, which are ready-made code libraries, retry for you."),
            leo("Anything else people get wrong?"),
            maya("Using the beta endpoint in production. Beta is the preview version, and it can change under you. Use version one point oh for real apps. And forgetting ConsistencyLevel: eventual with count or search."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four quick rules. If a call returns only part of a list, follow the next link. If you need many calls in few round trips, batch them, twenty per batch, so thirty is two. If the query uses count or search, add ConsistencyLevel: eventual. And if you get a 429, wait for Retry-After."),
            leo("And if a question is about the token?"),
            maya("Its audience must be Graph, not your own API. And production uses version one point oh, never beta. [pause] Shape the request first with select, filter and top, then page through."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Open Graph Explorer, no setup needed. Get yourself. Then get your top three messages, selecting only the subject. Then list users with a count, adding the ConsistencyLevel header. And look at the permissions tab for each call."),
            leo("One header, two batches, zero excuses. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val keyVault = Podcast(
        "az-keyvault",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about keeping secrets safe, things like passwords, or the connection string your app uses to reach its database. Here's a puzzle to start. Your app needs a password to open a locked box. Where should the app keep that password?"),
            leo("In a settings file? [pause] Hmm, no. Then the settings file is the thing I have to protect. That just moves the problem."),
            maya("[chuckle] Exactly. And the surprising answer is, nowhere. The app doesn't keep one at all. We'll get to how. First, the box itself. It's called Azure Key Vault."),
            leo("What is it, in plain words?"),
            maya("A safe in the cloud. You put your sensitive things in it, and only the people and apps you allow can take them out. Say you're building a small recipe app that keeps its users in a database. The password to that database is exactly the kind of thing that belongs in the vault. It holds three kinds of thing."),
            leo("Go on."),
            maya("First, secrets. A secret is any text you want hidden, like a password or a connection string. You can read a secret back, and the vault keeps the old versions when you change it. Second, keys. A key is for scrambling and unscrambling data, which is called encryption."),
            leo("Can I read a key back, like a secret?"),
            maya("No, and that's the important part. A key never leaves the vault. You send your data in, the vault does the scrambling inside, and sends you the result. For example, a notes app that protects each user's notes would send the notes to the vault to be scrambled. The key itself stays locked up."),
            leo("Like a notary who stamps your papers but never lends you the stamp."),
            maya("[laugh] That's perfect. Third, certificates, the files behind the little padlock in your browser. The vault can even renew some of them for you, with a supported certificate authority."),
            leo("Are all vaults the same?"),
            maya("There are two tiers, which is Azure's word for price levels. Standard protects keys in software. Premium keeps them in special tamper-proof hardware, called an HSM. A hobby app is fine on Standard. A bank's app that must keep its keys in hardware needs Premium."),
            leo("Okay. So who's allowed to open the safe?"),
            maya("You hand out roles. A role is a named permission, a bit like a job title. The recommended way is role-based access control, RBAC for short. For example, the role Key Vault Secrets User can read secrets and nothing more. Always give the smallest role that does the job, not Key Vault Administrator."),
            leo("And if someone deletes a secret by mistake?"),
            maya("There's a safety net. Soft delete is on by default, so a deleted secret goes into a kind of recycle bin for a while, and you can bring it back. Purge protection locks that bin, so nobody, not even an administrator, can empty it early."),
            leo("Right. Back to the puzzle, then. How does the app open the safe with no password?"),
            maya("With a managed identity. Think of it as an employee badge that Azure makes for your app. The app shows its badge, and the vault checks whether that badge has a role. There's no password anywhere for you to store, leak or change. Azure looks after it."),
            leo("[laugh] A badge instead of a password. I like that."),
            maya("And badges come in two kinds. A system-assigned identity belongs to one app only. You switch it on with one click, and when that app is deleted, the badge goes with it. Your recipe app on its own? A system-assigned badge is perfect."),
            leo("And the other kind?"),
            maya("A user-assigned identity. That's a badge you create on its own, first, and then hand to as many apps as you like. You give it its role once, and every app wearing it gets in."),
            leo("When would I want that?"),
            maya("Picture a small online shop with a website, a mobile backend and a nightly report job, all reading the same vault. Make one user-assigned badge, give it its role once, and hand it to all three. And because it exists on its own, you can set it up before any of those apps are even deployed."),
            leo("What does the code look like? Is it hard?"),
            maya("It's short. You use something called DefaultAzureCredential, and it's clever. On your laptop it uses the account you signed in with, by running az login. In Azure it uses the app's managed identity. The same code works in both places."),
            leo("And if I don't want to write any code at all?"),
            maya("Then use a Key Vault reference. It's a special value you put in your app's settings that says, fetch this from the vault. When the app starts, Azure fetches the secret for you, and your app just sees a normal setting. It's great for a simple website that only needs one connection string."),
            leo("So with a reference I can skip giving out the role?"),
            maya("No, and that's a common trap. The badge still needs its role on the vault. The reference only saves you the code."),
            leo("[sigh] Okay. What do people usually get wrong?"),
            maya("Four things. Giving the app the administrator role when it only reads. Switching on the badge but forgetting the role, which gives you an access denied error, a 403. Pinning a reference to one old version of a secret, so updates never arrive. And deleting a shared badge while apps still use it."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four quick rules. If the keys must be hardware protected, pick the Premium tier. If nobody may delete a secret for good, pick purge protection. If several apps share one identity, or it must exist before them, pick user-assigned. And if an app only reads secrets, give it Key Vault Secrets User, never Administrator."),
            leo("And if a question says no credentials anywhere in the code?"),
            maya("Then the answer is a managed identity, with DefaultAzureCredential in code, or a Key Vault reference in settings. [pause] That one idea comes up again and again."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Create a vault that uses roles, and add a secret. Give yourself the Secrets User role, and read the secret back from the command line. Then create a function app, switch on its system identity, add a Key Vault reference setting, and look for the green tick in the portal that says it resolves."),
            leo("Green tick. My favourite kind of tick. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val appConfig = Podcast(
        "az-appconfig",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is Azure App Configuration. Here's a puzzle to start. You change a setting, and your running app must notice without restarting. How many settings do you think it has to keep checking?"),
            leo("All of them? [pause] That sounds like an awful lot of checking."),
            maya("[chuckle] It would be. The answer is just one, and we'll see why. First, what App Configuration is."),
            leo("What is it, in plain words?"),
            maya("A shared notice board for settings. Things like limits, web addresses, colours, and on-off switches. All your apps read from the one board. You change a value once, and every copy of every app picks it up. Each setting is a key, which is its name, and a value."),
            leo("Why not just keep the settings inside each app?"),
            maya("Then every app has its own copy, and the copies drift apart. Say you're building a small online shop with a website, a mobile backend and an order service. All three need the same daily order limit. Put it on the board once, instead of in three places."),
            leo("Can I keep passwords there too?"),
            maya("No, and that's the first mistake on the list. The board is for settings that are not secrets. For a secret, store a Key Vault reference, which is a pointer to the secret in Key Vault. The app follows the pointer with its own identity, and the secret stays in the vault."),
            leo("What if my test app and my live app need different values?"),
            maya("That's what a label is for. A label is a variant of the same key, usually one per environment, like dev or prod. In the topic's example, the dev label sets the daily limit to fifty for testing, while prod keeps it at fifteen. Same key, two labels."),
            leo("And how does the app know which one to read?"),
            maya("It asks for its own label when it loads, using a label filter. The dev app reads dev, the live app reads prod. There's also a content type, which says how to read a value, like JSON for something structured. And a snapshot, a frozen, named set of keys, like everything exactly as it was for one release."),
            leo("Like a photo of the board on launch day."),
            maya("[laugh] Exactly that. And like a photo, a snapshot can't change afterwards."),
            leo("Okay. Back to the puzzle. Why only one key?"),
            maya("It's called a sentinel key. A sentinel is a guard on watch. The app watches just that one key. When you've finished changing settings, you bump the sentinel, and the app reloads every other key in one go. One request instead of hundreds."),
            leo("Who does the watching? A timer running in the background?"),
            maya("No. It's done by the middleware, a small piece of code that runs on each incoming request. After the refresh interval, the next request triggers the check. So forgetting to add the App Configuration middleware is another mistake. Without it, the refresh never happens."),
            leo("And registering every single key for refresh, just to be safe?"),
            maya("That's exactly what the sentinel pattern exists to avoid. Register the sentinel, and let it refresh everything. And your app code doesn't change at all. It reads the values just as it always did."),
            leo("Nice. Now, feature flags. What's a flag?"),
            maya("A feature flag is an on-off switch for one piece of your app, flipped from the board, with no new release. For example, a to-do app gets a new dark mode. You ship the code with the flag off, and switch it on when you're ready. If something breaks, switch it off again."),
            leo("Can a flag be on for some people and off for others?"),
            maya("Yes, with a feature filter. That's a rule that decides the flag's answer while the app runs. A percentage filter turns it on for a steady share of users. A time window filter turns it on between two times. A targeting filter picks particular users and groups. And you can write custom ones too."),
            leo("Give me an example of each."),
            maya("Picture a small recipe app with a new search. Show it to ten percent of users first? Percentage filter. A holiday banner that should only run over one weekend? Time window. Let your beta testers try it before anyone else? Targeting, with a beta group."),
            leo("And how does my code check a flag?"),
            maya("Through the feature manager. In C sharp, you ask it whether a flag is enabled. In a Razor web page there's a feature tag. On a controller there's a feature gate. And variants let a flag return a value, not just on or off, which is handy for A B tests."),
            leo("How does the app get into the store? With a connection string?"),
            maya("Better with its managed identity, the badge Azure gives your app, holding the App Configuration Data Reader role. Using a connection string when the app already has an identity is one more mistake. And there's a Free tier, one store per subscription with a thousand requests a day. Standard is for production."),
            leo("[sigh] Identity again. So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four quick rules. If settings must update without a restart, watch one sentinel key, with the middleware. If one key needs different values per environment, use a label. If a value is a secret, store a Key Vault reference, never the secret itself."),
            leo("And for flags?"),
            maya("If a feature goes to a share of users, like a fifth of them, that's a percentage filter. Not a label, and not a sentinel. Between two dates is a time window. Named users or groups is targeting. [pause] And the app reads the store with its identity and the Data Reader role."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("The free tier is enough. Create a free store, with two labelled values for one key. Add a feature flag, and toggle it. In a console app, load with Select and a label filter, and print the value. Then change the value in the portal, and add a sentinel refresh."),
            leo("One sentinel, many settings. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val blobBasics = Podcast(
        "az-blob-basics",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is Blob Storage. Here's a puzzle to start. A file sits in the cheapest storage Azure has, called Archive, and your app asks to read it. What happens?"),
            leo("It's read, just a bit slowly? [pause] Cheap things are usually slow."),
            maya("[chuckle] That's what most people guess. The real answer is a surprise, and we'll get there. First, what Blob Storage is."),
            leo("What is it, in plain words?"),
            maya("A place in the cloud to keep files of any size. Images, backups, logs, exports. Blob just means a file, of any kind. You pay for what you store, and for how often you touch it. There's no limit on how many files you keep."),
            leo("How is it organised?"),
            maya("In three layers. A storage account is the building. A container is a room inside it, a folder-like group of blobs with its own access level, like private. And the blob is the file itself. The account decides how many copies are kept, and the container decides who can get in."),
            leo("When would a small app use it?"),
            maya("Say you're building a small photo-sharing app. The users' photos go in a container called photos. Nightly backups of your database go in another container, called backups. One storage account, two rooms. And the photos container stays private, so only your app can get in."),
            leo("Are all blobs the same kind?"),
            maya("There are three types. A block blob is the default, for files you upload whole, like those photos. An append blob is for logs. You can only add to the end, never edit, like a diary written in pen. And a page blob is for random-access disks, the kind that sit behind virtual machines."),
            leo("So an app that writes a log line every second, and never changes the old lines?"),
            maya("Append blob. [pause] Now, copies. Azure always keeps several copies of your data, and the letters LRS, ZRS and GRS tell you how many copies, and how far apart."),
            leo("[laugh] Alphabet soup. Start me off."),
            maya("LRS, locally redundant, is three copies in one datacenter, which is one big building full of computers. It survives a disk failing. ZRS, zone redundant, spreads three copies across zones, separate buildings in the same region. It survives a whole building going down."),
            leo("And if the whole region goes?"),
            maya("GRS, geo redundant. That's LRS plus three more copies in a paired region, far away. But you can only read that second copy after a failover, a switch-over. If you must read it at any time, that's RA-GRS, read access GRS. And GZRS combines zones with geo copies."),
            leo("So which one do I pick?"),
            maya("For example, a hobby app's test files are fine on cheap LRS. A small shop that can't lose orders when one building fails wants ZRS. And records that must survive losing a whole region want GRS."),
            leo("Now, hot and cold. I keep hearing those words."),
            maya("That's the access tier. The idea is that you pay either for keeping a file or for reading it, never cheaply for both. Hot costs the most to keep and the least to read, with no minimum stay. Cool is cheaper to keep, dearer to read, with a thirty day minimum. Cold is cheaper again, ninety days."),
            leo("And Archive, from the puzzle?"),
            maya("Archive is the cheapest to keep, with a hundred and eighty days minimum. And here's the answer. [pause] An Archive blob is offline. You cannot read it at all. Not slowly, and not for extra money. The file is still there, still costing almost nothing to keep, but a read simply fails."),
            leo("Then how do I get it back?"),
            maya("You rehydrate it, which means moving it back to Hot or Cool, like adding water to dried food. On standard priority, that can take up to fifteen hours. Hot, Cool and Cold can all be read straight away, at different prices. Archive is the one that can't."),
            leo("[sigh] Fifteen hours. So when should I use which tier?"),
            maya("Picture a small study app that saves a report every night. This month's reports are read daily, so Hot. Older ones are kept for a year in case someone asks, so Cool. After that they're kept five years just for the record, so Archive."),
            leo("And who moves them? Surely not a person."),
            maya("A lifecycle rule. It's a policy that moves or deletes blobs by age. Cool after thirty days, Archive after a year, deleted after six years. It runs once a day, and nobody touches it by hand. You write the rule once, and it keeps the bill small for years."),
            leo("Nice. What do people get wrong?"),
            maya("Four things. Trying to read an Archive blob directly. Moving data to Cool and deleting it after a week, so an early deletion fee applies. Picking GRS when the question says read the secondary any time, which needs RA-GRS. And creating an old style account, then wondering where the tiers went. You need StorageV2."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four quick rules. If a blob is in Archive, it must be rehydrated before anyone can read it, and that takes hours. If data must survive losing a region, pick GRS. If it must also be readable from the second region without a failover, RA-GRS. And the cheapest with zone resilience is ZRS."),
            leo("And for the files themselves?"),
            maya("Add-only logs mean an append blob. Moving or deleting files by age means a lifecycle rule. Monthly reports kept a while suit Cool. [pause] And tiers need a StorageV2 account."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Create a StorageV2 account with LRS. Upload a file to a private container. Change its tier to Cool in the portal. Add a lifecycle rule that deletes after one day. Then delete the resource group, so nothing keeps costing you."),
            leo("Done before the kettle boils. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val blobSdk = Podcast(
        "az-blob-sdk",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Last time we met Blob Storage, a place in the cloud for files like photos and reports. Today we work with those files from code. Here's a puzzle to start. Your app keeps its users' photos private. A friend should see one photo, for ten minutes only. How do you let them in?"),
            leo("Give them the storage password? [pause] No. Then they could see everything, forever."),
            maya("[chuckle] Exactly. We'll solve it properly in a bit. First, the code itself. Azure gives you an SDK, which is a ready-made library for talking to a service. For blobs, it has three objects."),
            leo("Three objects. What are they, in plain words?"),
            maya("Think of a building. A service client is the whole building, your storage account. A container client is one room, a container. And a blob client is one box in that room, one file. You start at the building and walk in. Room, then box."),
            leo("And how does the code get through the front door? With a key?"),
            maya("No key. You use DefaultAzureCredential, the same helper from the Key Vault episode. On your laptop it uses your own az login. In Azure it uses the app's managed identity, its badge, which needs the Storage Blob Data Contributor role. Same code in both places, and no connection string anywhere."),
            leo("[laugh] No keys again. I'm seeing a theme in this course."),
            maya("It's the theme of the whole security side. Now, information about a file. Some of it Azure keeps for you, called system properties, like the file type and when it last changed. And some you add yourself. That's metadata."),
            leo("Metadata. What is that, really?"),
            maya("Little name and value labels stuck on the file, like a luggage tag. Owner is Usaid, week is forty-one. Up to eight kilobytes, and it comes back every time you read the file. For example, a photo-sharing app that stores who uploaded each picture would use metadata."),
            leo("So what if I want every photo uploaded by Usaid, across the whole account?"),
            maya("Then metadata can't help. You can read it on one file, but you can't search across it. For searching, you use index tags. They're also name and value pairs, but Azure keeps a list of them, like a library catalogue. So you can ask, show me every file where owner is Usaid."),
            leo("Luggage tag for one file, catalogue for searching. Got it."),
            maya("Now, the puzzle. The answer is a SAS, a shared access signature. In plain words, it's a link with limits. Like a hotel key card. It opens one door, does only what it allows, like read, and it stops working at checkout time."),
            leo("Are there different kinds of key card?"),
            maya("Three. A user delegation SAS covers blobs only, and it's signed through an Entra ID sign-in, using a user delegation key. A service SAS covers one service, like blobs or queues. And an account SAS can cover several services. Those last two are signed with the account key, the master key to the whole account."),
            leo("Which one should I pick?"),
            maya("The user delegation SAS. The master key never leaves the safe, and if something goes wrong, you can cancel the cards by revoking the user delegation key. With an account key SAS, the only way to cancel it is to change the master key, for everything that uses it."),
            leo("[sigh] Changing the master key for everything sounds painful."),
            maya("It is. There's one middle path. A stored access policy lets you cancel a service SAS without changing the key. But the safe default is a short user delegation SAS."),
            leo("So walk me through a real one."),
            maya("Say you're building a small photo app. Your API first checks that this user may see the photo. Storage doesn't decide that, your code does. Then it makes a SAS with read only, lasting thirty minutes, and returns the full link. The phone downloads straight from storage."),
            leo("And after thirty minutes?"),
            maya("The link stops working, and storage answers with a 403, which means access denied. The app just asks the API for a fresh link. A short life keeps the damage small if a link ever leaks."),
            leo("What about uploads from the phone? Can I put the key inside the app?"),
            maya("Never. That's the worst mistake on the list, because anyone can dig a key out of an app. Upload through your API, or hand out a short SAS. And don't give write permission when read is enough."),
            leo("Okay. Last piece. Leases."),
            maya("A lease is a lock on a file, so only one writer at a time. Like the engaged sign on a bathroom door. It lasts fifteen to sixty seconds, or forever until you release it."),
            leo("When would a simple app need that?"),
            maya("Picture a small game with three servers, all updating one shared file that holds the high score. Without a lease, two of them write at once and one change gets lost. With a lease, they take turns. A shared settings file is the same story."),
            leo("Any catch?"),
            maya("[laugh] Yes. A lease blocks your own writes too, unless you pass the lease id. People lock themselves out of their own bathroom."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four rules. If the question says store the uploader's name with the file, pick metadata. If it says search blobs by a key across the account, pick index tags. If it says give a browser short access to one private blob, pick a user delegation SAS. And if it says one writer at a time, pick a lease."),
            leo("And if it says no keys in the code?"),
            maya("Then DefaultAzureCredential with a managed identity, and never the account key inside a mobile app. [pause] That idea keeps coming back."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("You need the Azure CLI and the data contributor role. Upload a text blob, signing in with your own login. Set metadata on it. Generate a ten-minute read SAS as yourself, so it's a user delegation SAS. Open the URL in a browser, then open it again after it expires."),
            leo("A link with an expiry date. Like milk. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val cosmosModel = Podcast(
        "az-cosmos-model",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today we meet Cosmos DB, Azure's big database. Here's a puzzle to start. You built a chat app, it's live, and you realise you picked the wrong way to split up its data. Can you just fix it?"),
            leo("Change a setting, surely? [pause] You're smiling. That means no."),
            maya("[chuckle] It means no. We'll see why. First, what is Cosmos DB, in plain words? It's a database that stores JSON documents. A JSON document is just a little text record, like a contact card with a name, an email and a phone number. Cosmos copies them to any region you pick, and answers in a few milliseconds."),
            leo("And how is it organised?"),
            maya("Like a filing cabinet. The account is the whole cabinet. A database is one drawer. A container is a folder in that drawer. And items are the cards in the folder, your documents."),
            leo("Is there only one way to talk to it?"),
            maya("No. You pick an API when you create the account. An API here just means the language your code speaks to the database. NoSQL is the main one, for new apps with JSON and SQL-like queries. It's the exam's default. MongoDB is for apps already written for Mongo. And PostgreSQL, Cassandra, Gremlin and Table are mostly for moving existing apps in."),
            leo("Can I switch later?"),
            maya("No, the API is fixed when the account is created. Say you're moving a small shop app that already uses MongoDB, and the question says minimal code changes. Pick the MongoDB API, not NoSQL. A brand-new to-do app? NoSQL."),
            leo("[sigh] Fixed things. I sense a theme for today."),
            maya("There's another one coming. But first, consistency. Cosmos keeps copies of your data in several places, called replicas. A consistency level is the promise about how fresh a read is. Picture a news board copied to many towns. How quickly does every town see the latest news?"),
            leo("And what are the choices?"),
            maya("Five, from strict to relaxed. Strong means you always see the very latest write. It's the slowest, and costs the most. Bounded staleness means you might lag behind, but only by a set number of versions or seconds. Session means you always see your own writes."),
            leo("And the other two?"),
            maya("Consistent prefix means you might be a little behind, but never out of order. And eventual means everyone catches up in the end, in any order. It's the cheapest and fastest."),
            leo("Which one do I get if I don't choose?"),
            maya("Session. It's what most apps want. For example, in a notes app you see your own edit straight away, and other people see it soon after, for far less than Strong. Using Strong everywhere is a mistake. It doubles the cost of reads."),
            leo("So a game leaderboard that can be a bit late, but never out of order?"),
            maya("Consistent prefix. Order, without the cost of Strong. [pause] Now, the exam's favourite. Partition keys."),
            leo("What is that, in plain words?"),
            maya("Think of a big library, sorted onto shelves by one label, say the author's name. The partition key is that label, one property on every item. All items with the same value sit together on one shelf, called a logical partition. One shelf holds at most twenty gigabytes."),
            leo("Why does it matter so much?"),
            maya("Because a question that names the shelf is cheap. Show me Usaid's items? Go to one shelf. A question that doesn't name it has to walk every shelf, which is slow and expensive. And here's the puzzle answer. The partition key is fixed when the container is created. A wrong key means moving everything to a new container."),
            leo("So how do I choose well?"),
            maya("Three things, all at once. Lots of different values. Writes spread evenly across them. And it's in the question you ask most. For a study app where most questions are per user, the owner, a user id, is a great key."),
            leo("What about date, or country?"),
            maya("Date spreads out over time, but today's shelf gets every new write, and per-user questions walk every shelf. Country, never. Only a few values, so one huge shelf takes all the writes. That's called a hot partition."),
            leo("And back to my chat app from the start?"),
            maya("Picture a chat app where nearly every question asks for one conversation. Use the conversation id as the key, so each question hits one shelf. The message id would spread perfectly, but then every question would walk every shelf."),
            leo("Now, money. How do I pay for all this?"),
            maya("In request units, RU for short. Think of them as tokens at an arcade. Every action costs tokens. Reading one small item, about one kilobyte, by its id costs about one RU. A query costs more, and a write costs about five per kilobyte."),
            leo("And how do I buy the tokens?"),
            maya("Three ways. Provisioned means a fixed number per second, paid whether you use it or not. Good for steady, busy traffic. Autoscale sets a top limit, and shrinks to ten percent when it's quiet. Serverless means you pay only for what you use. Perfect for a small hobby app with spiky traffic."),
            leo("And if I run out of tokens?"),
            maya("You get a 429, which means too many requests, slow down. Retry after a short wait, or raise the limit. Ignoring 429s is a mistake."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Five rules. If it says minimal changes from MongoDB, pick the MongoDB API. If it asks for the default consistency, it's Session. Late but never out of order is consistent prefix. If traffic is unpredictable and cost must follow usage, serverless or autoscale. And if you see a 429, retry with backoff or add RU."),
            leo("And the partition key?"),
            maya("Many values, spread evenly, and in your most common query. And it can't be changed later. [pause] If you remember one thing, make it that."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("The free tier gives a thousand RU a second and twenty-five gigabytes. Create a free-tier NoSQL account. Create a container with owner as its partition key. Insert two items with different owners in Data Explorer. Then query the items for one owner, and read the RU charge in the query stats."),
            leo("Three RU of pure satisfaction. See you next time."),
        ),
