    private val cosmosSdk = Podcast(
        "az-cosmos-sdk",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome to the very last episode. Today, Cosmos DB in code. Here's a puzzle to start. Two people open the same shopping list at the same moment. Both add an item and press save. What happens to the first person's change?"),
            leo("The second save wins, and the first item just... [pause] vanishes?"),
            maya("[chuckle] Without help, yes. We'll fix that soon. First, the code. The dot net SDK, the ready-made library, mirrors what we saw last time. A Cosmos client for the account, then a database, then a container."),
            leo("What's the cheapest thing I can do with it?"),
            maya("A point read. That means reading one item by its id plus its partition key. It's like knowing the shelf and the exact spot on it. You walk straight there, no searching. About one RU."),
            leo("And when I need to search?"),
            maya("Then you run a query, and you include the partition key, so it stays on one shelf. For example, a fitness app showing my workouts this week only looks on my own shelf. Running a query across every shelf when a point read would do is the first mistake on the list."),
            leo("Okay. How do I write things?"),
            maya("Create adds a new item. Upsert writes an item, or replaces it if it's already there. And patch changes just the fields you name, without sending the whole document back. Handy when you only bump a counter."),
            leo("Now, my shopping list. How do I stop that lost change?"),
            maya("With the ETag. Every item carries one. It's a version stamp, like the edition number on a newspaper. It changes every time the item is written."),
            leo("So what do I do with it?"),
            maya("When you save, you send back the ETag you read, with a condition called if-match. It means, only save if the item is still the edition I saw. If someone else saved first, your save fails with a 412. Then you read the item again, and apply your change once more."),
            leo("[laugh] So nobody locks the list?"),
            maya("No lock at all. That's called optimistic concurrency. You hope nobody clashes, and you check when you save. Not a stored procedure, and not a stronger consistency level. Just the stamp."),
            leo("You said stored procedure. What runs inside the database?"),
            maya("Four kinds of code, all JavaScript, all inside the database. A stored procedure runs inside one partition as a transaction, which means all or nothing. Say you're building a booking app that must save a booking and a payment record together. Either both land, or neither does."),
            leo("And the other three?"),
            maya("A pre-trigger runs before a write, to check it or add a timestamp. A post-trigger runs after a write, in the same transaction, say to update a counter. And a user-defined function runs inside a query, to format or work out a value."),
            leo("Do triggers just fire on their own?"),
            maya("No, and that's a trap. You must name the trigger in the request, or it doesn't run. And everything server-side works inside one partition key."),
            leo("Can items delete themselves after a while?"),
            maya("Yes, with a TTL, time to live. It's like a sell-by date, counted in seconds. Set a default on the container, or a TTL on each item, and Cosmos removes them for you. For example, a login code that should disappear after five minutes."),
            leo("Nice. Now, the change feed. What is it, in plain words?"),
            maya("A running list of every insert and update, in order, per partition. Like the activity log on a shared document. Something reads that list and reacts. Usually a function with a Cosmos DB trigger, or the change feed processor in the SDK."),
            leo("React how?"),
            maya("Picture a study app with a streak counter for each user. Instead of recounting every time the page loads, a function watches the change feed. For each new study log, it updates the streak. It could also send a notification, or copy data to another store."),
            leo("Does it see deletes too?"),
            maya("[sigh] That's the catch. Deletes are not in the change feed, unless you turn on a preview mode called all versions and deletes. So to react to a removal, you mark the item as deleted, a soft delete, often with a TTL."),
            leo("And how does the function remember where it got to?"),
            maya("A lease container. It's a small container, partitioned by id, that works like a bookmark. It stores checkpoints, how far each copy of the function has read, so two copies never handle the same change."),
            leo("Can two different functions share one bookmark container?"),
            maya("Only if each gets its own prefix, a short name tag on its bookmarks. Sharing without a prefix is a mistake. And to replay everything from the start, set start from beginning, and every existing item comes through once."),
            leo("So what should I remember for the exam?"),
            maya("Four rules. If it says the cheapest read, pick a point read by id and partition key. If two writers must not overwrite each other, pick the ETag with if-match. If several items must be saved all or nothing, pick a stored procedure. And if it says react to every write, pick the change feed, with a lease container."),
            leo("Any traps to listen for?"),
            maya("Two. Triggers only run when the request names them. And deletes are not in the change feed. [pause] Spot those, and you've earned easy points."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Use the free-tier account from last time. Insert an item from dot net with CreateItemAsync. Read it back with ReadItemAsync, and print the RequestCharge. Run the same as a query without the partition key, and compare the charge. Then give an item a TTL of sixty, and watch it vanish."),
            leo("An item that vanishes in a minute. Last episode, and still dramatic. Good luck in the exam, and see you next time."),
        ),
    )

